	abstract = {This study investigates the dynamic relationships between maternal implicit love, explicit love, and children’s perceptions of maternal love, as well as the interplay of maternal and child background factors that shape these processes. Grounded in prior literature, maternal love is conceptualized as comprising two dimensions: Implicit maternal love, which reflects a mother’s inner emotions, sacrifice, will, and expectations toward her children; and explicit maternal love, which is expressed through concrete behaviors such as caregiving, proximity, and compensatory actions. Children’s perception of maternal love represents their subjective appraisal of how these implicit and explicit dimensions are conveyed and experienced. The present research not only compares maternal self-reports with children’s perceptions but also explores how maternal education, marital and economic difficulties, children’s gender, birth order, and behavioral performance interact to influence the transmission and reception of maternal love. A total of 427 matched mother–child dyads from junior and senior high schools across Taiwan were recruited through stratified regional sampling. The Maternal Love Scale (Huang, 2019–2021) and the Perceived Maternal Love Scale adapted for adolescents were employed, both with robust reliability and validity indices (CR =.83–.95; AVE =.70–.80). Maternal background factors (education, marital difficulty, economic stress) and children’s factors (gender, age, birth order, self- and mother-reported performance, perceptions of maternal partiality, discipline, and companionship) were included. Structural equation modeling using PLS-SEM was applied to examine hypothesized relationships and mediation effects. The results revealed several significant findings. First, mothers reported significantly higher levels of both implicit and explicit love than those perceived by their children, indicating a substantial perception gap. Except for one compensatory item, mothers’ self-rated love consistently exceeded children’s perceptions across emotional, sacrificial, caregiving, and intimacy domains. This gap suggests that while mothers believe they provide abundant love, children may not fully internalize or recognize these expressions, especially in routine caregiving or disciplinary contexts. Second, implicit maternal love did not directly predict children’s perception of love (β = -0.011, ns). Instead, its influence was mediated by explicit maternal behaviors. Implicit love strongly predicted explicit caregiving and intimacy (β =.742, p <.001), and explicit love in turn predicted children’s perception of love (β =.476, p <.001). Thus, the transmission of maternal love is contingent on observable behaviors–children’s perceptions are shaped more by what mothers do than by what they feel internally. Third, children’s behavioral performance significantly influenced maternal love. Better child performance (obedience, filial piety, politeness, academic success, and manageability) predicted higher maternal implicit emotions and sacrifice (R<sup>2</sup> =.286), which further translated into stronger explicit caregiving and intimacy (R<sup>2</sup> =.348). Mediation analysis confirmed that child performance primarily affected explicit maternal love indirectly through implicit maternal love (VAF = 58%). This dynamic supports a reciprocal loop: children’s good performance enhances maternal love, which in turn reinforces children’s behavior, creating a positive cycle. Conversely, poorer performance risks reducing maternal emotional investment, potentially leading to a negative spiral. Fourth, maternal background factors significantly shaped love expressions. Higher maternal education predicted stronger emotional implicit love (β =.090, p <.05) and greater explicit intimacy (β =.088, p <.05), while economic difficulty increased sacrificial implicit love (β =.179, p <.01) but reduced explicit intimacy (β = -.121, p <.05). Marital difficulties lowered emotional implicit love (β = -.133, p =.003) but increased compensatory explicit behaviors (β =.183, p <.001), suggesting that mothers under relational stress may reduce emotional openness yet attempt to compensate behaviorally. These findings highlight the nuanced ways socio-economic and marital contexts shape maternal love. Fifth, children’s perception of maternal love was primarily predicted by explicit maternal behaviors. Maternal caregiving, intimacy, and compensatory acts all positively predicted children’s perceptions across corresponding domains. Moreover, maternal companionship (β =.576, p <.001) and perceived partiality (β =.134, p <.01) further enhanced children’s feelings of being loved, while maternal harsh discipline negatively affected perceptions of caregiving (β = -.081, p <.05). These results underscore that the quality and style of maternal behaviors—particularly companionship and avoidance of harsh discipline—play a pivotal role in how love is received. Finally, children’s perception of maternal love predicted their own behavioral performance. Specifically, perceived caregiving (β =.442, p <.001) and compensatory love (β =.121, p <.05) significantly improved children’s self-reported behaviors, explaining 32.3% of variance in performance. Perceived intimacy showed a near-significant effect (β =.108, p =.056), suggesting potential long-term impacts of relational closeness on behavior. This finding illustrates a bidirectional process: not only does children’s behavior influence maternal love, but children’s perception of being loved also shapes their conduct and adaptation. This study evaluated the explanatory power of the structural model using R<sup>2</sup> f<sup>2</sup> and Q<sup>2.</sup> The results showed that mothers’ explicit behaviors (caregiving provision, companionship, favoritism toward the child, and less harsh discipline) had the strongest explanatory power for children’s perception of maternal love (R<sup>2</sup> up to.579), and children’s perception of maternal love further significantly predicted their behavioral performance (R<sup>2</sup> =.323). In contrast, implicit maternal love demonstrated lower explanatory power (R<sup>2</sup> =.160–.267), suggesting that its influence may be moderated by other social or cultural factors. Effect size analysis further indicated that maternal emotions strongly affected explicit caregiving behaviors (f<sup>2</sup> =.842), while companionship had the most prominent effect on children’s perception of maternal love (f<sup>2</sup> =.561), underscoring the central role of companionship and emotional exchange in transmitting maternal love. Variables such as marital difficulties, education level, and harsh discipline had relatively weaker effects. Overall, mothers’ explicit behaviors were more influential than internal emotions in shaping children’s perception of maternal love, which in turn mediated their behavioral development. Beyond statistical findings, this study also emphasizes cultural and practical implications. In Western contexts, maternal love is often framed within attachment theory and expressed through verbal affection and physical closeness. In contrast, East Asian and particularly Taiwanese mothers historically emphasized action-oriented care, sacrifice, and provision, sometimes at the expense of verbal or physical expressions. Our findings indicate that while these culturally rooted practices persist, children today interpret and value explicit intimacy—such as hugs, verbal affirmations, and companionship—more strongly than before. This suggests a cultural transition: modern Taiwanese adolescents may increasingly expect maternal love to be expressed not only through sacrifice and provision but also through relational closeness, paralleling Western norms. Practically, the findings carry important implications for family education, parenting guidance, and social policy. Parenting programs should encourage mothers to supplement sacrifice and provision with consistent emotional and behavioral expressions—especially companionship and positive discipline strategies. Family education curricula could incorporate training for mothers to recognize perception gaps and to practice explicit verbal and non-verbal demonstrations of love. Policies should also address structural challenges: marital counseling, financial assistance, and flexible work-family policies could reduce the burdens that suppress maternal emotional investment. In doing so, society can support mothers in maintaining both the implicit emotional base and explicit behavioral expressions of love, ensuring healthier parent–child relationships and stronger developmental outcomes for children. This study extends maternal love research by combining implicit and explicit dimensions with children’s perceptions in a large-scale dyadic design, verified through advanced structural modeling. The results underscore the importance of observable maternal behaviors, companionship, and positive parent–child interaction patterns for fostering children’s emotional security and adaptive behaviors. Practical implications point to the need for family education and parenting programs that encourage mothers to balance emotional and behavioral expressions of love, reduce reliance on harsh discipline, and provide stable support regardless of children’s performance. Policy interventions offering educational, marital, and economic support for mothers are also vital to strengthen positive maternal love under stressful circumstances. In conclusion, maternal love in Taiwan is best understood as a dynamic, culturally embedded interplay between maternal inner emotions, behavioral expressions, and children’s subjective perceptions, all of which converge to shape both parenting practices and children’s developmental outcomes. © (2026), (National Taiwan Normal University). All rights reserved.},
	author_keywords = {explicit maternal love; implicit maternal love; maternal love; parent-child interaction; perception of maternal love; 關鍵詞：母愛、母愛感受、內隱母愛、外顯母愛、親子互動},
	correspondence_address = {S.-M. Huang; Department of Child and Family Science, National Taiwan Normal University, China; email: shumanwa@ntnu.edu.tw},
	publisher = {National Taiwan Normal University},
	issn = {10115714},
	language = {English},
	abbrev_source_title = {Bull. Educ. Psychol.},
	type = {Article},
	publication_stage = {Final},
	source = {Scopus},
	note = {Cited by: 0}
}

@ARTICLE{Yin2026,
	author = {Yin, Yuting and Liu, Binyi and Chang, Qing},
	title = {Predicting people perceived plant diversity in urban green spaces using panoramic photos: a case study of Shanghai},
	year = {2026},
	journal = {Frontiers in Forests and Global Change},
	volume = {9},
	pages = {},
	doi = {10.3389/ffgc.2026.1732188},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105040348486?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Introduction – Urban green spaces (UGSs) play a dual role in high-density cities: they are crucial for biodiversity conservation and serve as key venues for promoting residents’ health. However, the benefits gained by people in these settings depend less on objectively measured biodiversity and more on their subjective perception of it. Visual cues, which account for over 80% of sensory input, significantly shape this perception. Methods – This study explores the interrelationships among measured plant diversity, perceived plant diversity, and visual landscape characteristics in UGSs, so that to develop a predictive model for perceived diversity using the other two variables. Based on a case study of nine representative parks in Shanghai, the research compared measured plant diversity—using four indices (arbor, shrub, herbaceous, and community diversity)—with perceived plant diversity and evaluated the influence of visual landscape features. Results – Results showed a significant mismatch between measured and perceived diversity. Furthermore, visual characteristics were more effective than measured biodiversity in predicting perceived plant diversity. These findings offer practical, short-term design strategies for enhancing perceived biodiversity in urban parks—complementing longer-term ecological measures aimed at increasing actual biodiversity. Discussion – This study advances the understanding of how objective biodiversity, human perception, and visual environment interact, and supports the design of nature-based solutions that benefit both human well-being and biodiversity conservation. Copyright © 2026 Yin, Liu and Chang.},
	author_keywords = {human perception; plant diversity; prediction models; semantic segmentation; urban green spaces},
	correspondence_address = {B. Liu; email: byltjulk@vip.sina.com},
	publisher = {Frontiers Media SA},
	issn = {2624893X},
	language = {English},
	abbrev_source_title = {Front. For. Glob. Change},
	type = {Article},
	publication_stage = {Final},
	source = {Scopus},
	note = {Cited by: 1; All Open Access; Gold Open Access}
}

