	affiliations = {Department of Sociology and Anthropology, Utah State University, Logan, UT, United States; Department of Sociology, Gettysburg College, Gettysburg, PA, United States},
	abstract = {Iconic architecture can be used to broadcast national identity. Such buildings tend to be rejected by the public initially, but urban landscaping involves a long-term and multifaceted process of promoting the symbolic and cultural values of space. Drawing on interviews with 122 tourists and residents, we examine how the iconic landscape of Marina Bay, Singapore, has been folded into public perceptions of the country. For several reasons, the public has resisted seeing Marina Bay in the eyes of architects and urban planners: (1) participants did not see it as a place that represents everyday life in Singapore; (2) Marina Bay’s newness contradicted the notion that identity is rooted in culture; and (3) they distanced Singaporean identity from what they considered to be the spread of global culture. These concerns relate to culture being perceived as traditional while the urban environment in Singapore continues to be fast changing. Still, some participants saw Marina Bay as part of an evolving national identity. We question how much time iconic spaces need to become incorporated into perceptions of the nation. If Marina Bay can stand the test of time, it could, perhaps, be folded into Singapore’s national identity, especially for new generations. © 2025 Informa UK Limited, trading as Taylor & Francis Group.},
