	affiliations = {University of the Basque Country EHU, Spain},
	abstract = {This study examines preservice teachers’ attitudes towards gender equality at the University of the Basque Country (UPV/EHU), focusing on differences between women and men across three dimensions of gender construction: sociocultural, relational, and personal. The sample consisted of 230 preservice teachers (174 women, 56 men; M = 21.3 years) enrolled in Early Childhood and Primary Education. Attitudes were assessed using an adapted version of García-Pérez et al.’s (2010) scale, comprising 50 items rated on a 5-point Likert scale. Data were analysed through one-way analyses of variance (ANOVA), which revealed significant gender differences in six items of the sociocultural subscale (e.g., attitudes towards sexist content in teaching materials, use of inclusive language, perceptions of driving ability, and views on gender equality in science and sports), five items of the relational subscale (e.g., comfort with same-sex displays of affection, relational jealousy), and two items of the personal subscale (e.g., silence when women are insulted, acceptance of women dressing in traditionally masculine styles). Across dimensions, women generally demonstrated more egalitarian attitudes than men. These findings highlight the persistence of ambivalent sexism in teacher education and underscore the need to embed transversal gender equality training into preservice teacher curricula to promote inclusive educational practices. © 2026 Hipatia Editorial. All rights reserved.},
