	affiliations = {Department of Geography, Trichandra Multiple Campus, Tribhuvan University, Kathmandu, Nepal; Professor of Geography, Tribhuvan University, Kathmandu and Former Chairperson, University Grants Commission, Nepal},
	abstract = {Ensuring equitable accessibility to Public Open Spaces (POS) is essential for fostering an inclusive and livable urban environment. Metropolitan cities in developing nations are facing challenges in this direction. This paper analyzes POS accessibility in Kathmandu, incorporating both physical proximity and people's perceptions. Using a mixed-methods approach, the study combines GIS-based proximity and space syntax analysis with data from 354 surveys and 54 interviews to provide a robust picture of POS accessibility. Results of physical proximity analysis reveal that POS in core areas are more accessible compared to outer areas. However, the users' survey and interview data reveal that perceived accessibility is often better in outer areas. The city core is inhabited by longtime residents whose social, cultural, institutional, and behavioral spatial practices are closely linked to nearby open spaces, often making these areas feel less accessible to outsiders. The study concludes that integrating objective analysis with subjective user perceptions provides a more comprehensive understanding of POS accessibility, offering valuable insights and setting the agenda for future research aimed at promoting livable urban development and sustainability initiatives in rapidly developing cities of the Global South. © 2026 The Authors.},
