	affiliations = {Faculty of Architecture and Urban Planning, Chongqing University, Chongqing, 400030, China; Key Laboratory of New Technology for Construction of Cities in Mountain Areas, Chongqing University, 400045, China},
	abstract = {Urban biodiversity is increasingly recognized as an important component of high-quality urban green spaces, yet its ecological value does not automatically translate into public experiential benefits. This study develops a social–ecological framework of ecological availability, perceptual legibility and benefit formation to explain how measured biodiversity may acquire experiential relevance through visitors’ perceptions of biodiversity. We empirically tested this framework in a mountainous urban park in Chongqing, China, by integrating ecological surveys and visitor questionnaires, with preliminary SOPARC-based behavioural observations used to inform plot selection and characterize recreational context. The analysis linked measured plant and bird species richness, sensory landscape cues, perceived species richness and four experiential attributes related to recreation and restoration: serenity, shelter, spaciousness and prospect. Results showed that measured and perceived biodiversity were related but not interchangeable, with taxonomic and scale-dependent discrepancies. Among the sensory cues, perceived birdsong diversity substantially improved the explanation of perceived species richness, indicating that ecological features are more likely to enter visitors’ experience when they become audible, visible or otherwise perceptible. Measured biodiversity had limited direct explanatory power for recreational and restorative attributes, whereas perceived species richness was most clearly associated with serenity. An exploratory indirect-effect analysis identified a small indirect contemporaneous association between measured biodiversity and serenity through perceived species richness, whereas the direct association was not significant. However, the concurrent measurement of perceived species richness and serenity precludes causal or temporal interpretation. These findings position perceived biodiversity as a potential social-ecological interface linking urban ecological quality with public experiential benefits. They suggest that biodiversity-oriented urban green-space research and management should consider not only ecological quality, but also the perceptual legibility of ecological features. Copyright © 2026. Published by Elsevier GmbH.},
