	abstract = {The purpose of the study is to provide a philosophical understanding of Central Asian nomadic thinking as an independent civilisational model through the analysis of the categorical foundations of perceptions of space, time, movement, and stability, reflected in symbolic forms and textual representations. Comparative philosophical, historical-anthropological, and textual approaches form the methodological basis of the study. As a result, the integrity of nomadic thinking is substantiated as a philosophical system grounded in the concepts of movement, rhythm, open space, and collective memory, which shape a specific ontology and structure of world perception. The internal foundations of nomadic civilisation are revealed, in which time is perceived through the cyclical nature of natural and ritual processes. Space is conceptualised as a sacred landscape, while sound functions as a key form of cognition and knowledge storage. It is established that nomadic society-maintained stability through symbolic regulators, oral tradition, and tribal organisation, without reliance on fixed institutions or territoriality. The comparison of external and internal representations substantiates nomadism as an alternative form of civilisational existence characterised by internal logic and coherence. © 2026, Polish Academy of Sciences - Institute of Philosophy and Sociology. All rights reserved.},
