	affiliations = {State Key Laboratory of Earth Surface Processes and Disaster Risk Reduction, Faculty of Geographical Science, Beijing Normal University, Beijing, 100875, China; Key Laboratory of Tibetan Plateau Land Surface Processes and Ecological Conservation, Qinghai Normal University, Xining, 810016, China},
	abstract = {With accelerating urbanization and rising living standards, urban parks have become increasingly prominent as crucial public welfare resources. Although emerging technologies enable new paradigms for park-related well-being research, the mechanisms through which their multidimensional features affect human well-being remain systematically underexplored. This study aims to reveal the key impact pathways of urban park features on various well-being dimensions and their spatiotemporal heterogeneity. We selected 179 representative parks across six categories within Beijing's six central districts and collected 167,173 relevant reviews during 2022–2023 from the Dianping network platform. Then we constructed a multilevel well-being lexicon containing 4310 characteristic words across seven dimensions using Word2Vec modeling. This lexicon enabled quantitative analysis of spatiotemporal variation in perceived well-being and systematic evaluation of internal/external service supply characteristics. Partial Least Squares Structural Equation Modeling (PLS-SEM) was employed to investigate the effects of various features on subjective and objective well-being. Results indicated pronounced temporal fluctuations and park-type dependencies in well-being perception. In-park ecosystem services (ES) and infrastructure diversity directly enhanced well-being, and landscape patterns indirectly promoted well-being by enhancing ES delivery. In contrast, outside features exhibited varying effects across different well-being dimensions. This research can help establish a novel methodology for human well-being assessment and provide evidence-based insights for urban park planning and sustainable development governance. © 2025 Ecological Society of China.},
