	affiliations = {School of Surveying and Geo-informatics, Shandong Jianzhu University, Shandong, Jinan, 250101, China},
	abstract = {Urban park quality is vital to residents' well-being and urban sustainability. Existing evaluations fail to effectively integrate physical environmental attributes with human usage experience, potentially disconnecting results from actual needs. To address this, this study combines remote sensing images and social media data to propose an evaluation method based on the synergy and differentiation of three core elements (landscape, behavior, emotion), using the Comprehensive Experience Index (CEI) to quantify comprehensive quality and applying it to 82 parks within Beijing's Sixth Ring Road. Results show: (1) Beijing's park CEI presents a “central-high, peripheral-low” pattern with phased evolution; 2017–2023 saw rapid improvement then stabilization, with notable enhancement inside the Third Ring Road and low quality outside the Fifth. (2) Landscape quality and satisfaction drive visitor volume but with spatial mismatch; high-quality parks concentrate inside the Second Ring Road, and different park types have non-linear differentiation. (3) Historical parks perform best in comprehensive quality; specialized parks are relatively poor, comprehensive parks have low visits due to location or diversion effects, and community parks have excellent landscapes but insufficient attractiveness. Suggestions include prioritizing resources for Third-Sixth Ring parks, optimizing comprehensive parks' accessibility and characteristics, and improving community parks' facilities and management. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd.},
