	affiliations = {School of Architecture and Design, Harbin Institute of Technology, Key Laboratory of Cold Region Urban and Rural Human Settlement Environment Science and Technology, Ministry of Industry and Information Technology, China},
	abstract = {Evaluation of streetscape perception is crucial for improving walkable urban environments and enhancing residents' physical and mental health. However, due to the influence of regional climate conditions, street environments vary significantly across locations and seasons. While street view images (SVIs) are commonly used to assess street quality, the impact of winter street environments on perception remains unexplored due to the scarcity of winter SVI data. This study proposes a winter streetscape perception assessment framework based on generative AI. A dataset of 500 winter and 6092 summer SVIs was compiled, and a CycleGAN model was trained to generate winter SVIs. Validation results showed that 80.11% of the generated winter SVIs were recognized as exhibiting authentic winter characteristics. An EfficientNet-B0 model was then developed to predict four types of perceptions: happy, relaxed, boring, and depressing. The R² of each perception type ranges from 0.82 to 0.87, which is highly consistent with human evaluations. Correlation analysis, OLS regression analysis, and MGWR analysis were conducted to examine the associations between winter streetscape elements and perception responses. The results illustrated that winter-specific features significantly influence perception and revealed how climatic conditions reshape the perception-element relationships. The findings also highlight the dual nature and spatial heterogeneity of individual streetscape elements, with perceptual effects varying according to street functions and regional climatic contexts. This research advances perception-based streetscape assessment by overcoming seasonal SVI data limitations, and provides a replicable, data-driven framework for climate-adaptive street environment design. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd},
	author_keywords = {Deep learning; Generative AI; Perception; Spatial heterogeneity; Street view images; Winter streetscape},
