	abstract = {Evidence shows that well-designed places can positively influence health and well-being, providing an opportunity to mitigate negative consequences that accompany increasing urbanisation. However, there is a gap in understanding of how the physical environment might support processes of place attachment to realise associated benefits like perceived psychological restoration, a sense of belonging, and improved community relations. This exploratory qualitative study used participant-employed photography to guide semi-structured interviews. Twenty participants’ understandings of the physical environment of their favourite urban places in a neighbourhood in north-west London, UK, were investigated. Inductive, reflexive thematic analysis of the interviews identified themes and subthemes including Relationship with Place, Afforded Experiences in Place, and Perception of the Physical Place. Common attributes (location, naturalness, spatial definition, variety) and features (places to sit, a view, points of focus, architectural features) were perceived by participants to afford meaningful experiences reflecting psychological processes of place attachment across the variety of favourite urban places. Insights are offered into the relationship between the physical environment and place attachment through an affordance perspective. With this understanding, positive place relationships can be better fostered as urban environments adapt to address increasing demand and environmental uncertainty. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd},
