	affiliations = {School of Geography, South China Normal University, Guangzhou, China; Research Center for Ecological Civilization and Green Governance, South China Normal University, Guangzhou, China; Division of Health Policy and Management, Korea University, Seoul, South Korea},
	abstract = {Housing affordability shapes community dynamics in urban environments. While conventional affordability measures rely on predetermined thresholds to identify housing cost burdens, such metrics fail to capture subjective housing experiences of public housing residents. Also, it remains unclear whether such renters develop perceived housing cost burdens despite being exempt from objective housing cost burdens, and how these perceived burdens affect community perceptions (specifically housing satisfaction, willingness to help neighbors, and NIMBY attitudes). To reduce knowledge gaps, this paper analyzes longitudinal data from 6,317 individuals in the Seoul Public Rental Housing Panel Survey (2016–2021) using individual fixed-effects models. Our results showed that higher perceived housing cost burden was associated with lower willingness to help neighbors and stronger NIMBY attitudes, while its association with housing satisfaction was weakly negative. In addition, the relationship between housing cost burden and NIMBYism was moderated by years in residence, with long-term residents showing attenuated negative responses. These findings indicate that housing cost burdens operate not only as financial stressors but also as drivers of community engagement and local attitudes. © 2026 Informa UK Limited, trading as Taylor & Francis Group.},
