	abstract = {Green spaces support well-being; however, green public open spaces (GPOS) in Yogyakarta are smaller than the recommended standard. Urban aging communities are also vulnerable to failure to embrace healthy aging. Studies on the roles of the small-scale GPOS in older people’s lives are still limited. To fill this research gap, this study explored the spatial roles of small-scale GPOS in supporting healthy aging in older people in dense urban kampung. Two GPOS located in the subdistricts with the highest percentage of older people in Yogyakarta were chosen as case studies. Fieldwork and semi-structured interviews with 60 older adults were used to obtain the data. The data were analyzed by investigating GPOS physical settings and older people’s activities and perceptions of the GPOS. This study found that the small-scale GPOS in dense urban kampung plays three beneficial roles for older people: (1) supplementing space for household chores, (2) providing social space, and (3) facilitating restorative space. Despite limited space, the GPOS’s roles in space fulfill spatial features in creating mobility opportunities that encourage healthy aging in older people. This study expands the existing knowledge concerning the design of urban GPOS and contributes to the conceptualization of spatial roles in small-scale GPOS. © 2026 The Author(s). Published by Informa UK Limited, trading as Taylor & Francis Group on behalf of the Architectural Institute of Japan, Architectural Institute of Korea and Architectural Society of China.},
