	affiliations = {College of Landscape Architecture, Sichuan Agricultural University, Chengdu, 611100, China; Chengdu Botanical Garden, China; Chengdu Baigongyan Greening Engineering Co., Ltd, China},
	abstract = {Urban forest trails serve as vital green infrastructure supporting public health and overall well-being in urban environments. However, the precise multisensory pathways by which perceived biodiversity fosters psychological restoration in urban forest settings remain to be elucidated. This study investigates the restorative effects of perceived biodiversity across four taxa, including woody plants, herbaceous plants, birds, and insects, using perceived restorativeness as a primary mediator. We conducted in-situ surveys at the Yangtianwo and Zhongjiashan trails within Longquan Mountain Urban Forest Park, China (Chengdu). Structural Equation Modeling (SEM) was employed to quantify the influence pathways of visual, auditory, and olfactory perceptions. The research results indicate that perceived biodiversity of woody plants and birds significantly promotes the actual individual psychological recovery outcome through the mediation of perceived restorativeness, with woody plants exerting the most substantial influence. Specifically, the color diversity of woody plants and the vocal diversity of birds are primary drivers of psychological restoration. Conversely, perceived herbaceous biodiversity is negatively correlated with restorative outcomes. No significant effects were observed for perceived insect biodiversity or participant demographics. Subjective multi-sensory experience serves as a vital bridge for environmental healing. To optimize urban forest trail design, planners should prioritize the chromatic configuration of woody plants and create bird-friendly soundscapes, while cautiously managing understory vegetation to avoid negative cognitive biases. © 2026 Elsevier GmbH.},
