	doi = {10.22194/JGIAS/26.1956},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105047200359?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {Laboratory of Botany and Biodiversity, Higher Institute of Agriculture and Entrepreneurship, Faculty of Science and Techniques, Cheikh Anta Diop University of Dakar, P.O. Box 5005, Dakar-Fann, Senegal},
	abstract = {In Senegal, ornamental plants and their associated weeds green areas remain understudied. However, promoting sustainability in urban green areas requires knowledge of the floristic composition of ornamental and associated species. This would necessarily involve providing precise information on the overall flora and on the biology and chorology of the species. The aim of this study is to assess the diversity of ornamental flora and associated weeds at UCAD’s Social Campus. An itinerant floristic inventory was conducted in the landscaped areas of the study area. The macrophyte flora of UCAD’s Social Campus is composed of 116 species belonging to 93 genera and 48 families, with a dominance of Eudicots in terms of species, genera and families. Microphanerophytes predominate accounting for 35.34% of the biological spectrum, followed by therophytes (18.97%) and nanophanerophytes (14.66%). Pantropical species represent almost half of the species recorded (44.74%). The associated weeds were dominated by therophytes (68.97%) and are mainly pantropical (48.15%). The most represented families are Arecaceae (9 species), Euphorbiaceae (8 species), Apocynaceae and Moraceae (7 species each). Species are primarily cultivated for their flowers (40.96%) and leaves (33.73%). The most common ornamental plants are Adonidia merrillii, Cocos nucifera, Codiaeum variegatum, Cynodon dactylon, Polyscias guilfoylei and Volkameria inermis. This study recommends assessing local perceptions of floristic composition to improve green space management. © 2026, Society for Innovative Agriculture, University of Agriculture. All rights reserved.},
