	abstract = {Outdoor Thermal Comfort (OTC) serves as a critical indicator for evaluating sustainable and livable urban environments, where establishing urban thermal benchmarks provides scientific guidance for optimizing outdoor activities during extreme temperature seasons. Although OTC is conventionally associated with urban spatial morphology and frequently simulated through climatic parameters, it is significantly influenced by personal, social, and psychological factors. This study integrated questionnaire surveys with field measurements to investigate summer OTC characteristics in 14 public spaces within the main urban area of Dalian, Liaoning Province, China, classified under Local Climate Zones (LCZ). The research aimed to identify thermal benchmarks in typical environments and elucidate dominant influencing factors. Key findings revealed: (1) Significant variations in thermal perception across built environments, with LCZ A and LCZ 4 demonstrating superior thermal comfort, while LCZ 1–2 (compact high-rise/mid-rise zones) exhibited pronounced thermal discomfort. (2) Distinct thermal benchmarks emerged among different built environments during summer, despite similarities within LCZ classifications. (3) LCZ 5 (open mid-rise zones) displays the highest Neutral Universal Thermal Climate Index (NUTCI) threshold (23.28 °C), contrasting with the minimum value of LCZ 4 (20.46 °C). (4) Thermal perception is shaped by multidimensional interactions among physical, psychological, social, and personal factors, with spatial variations in dominant influences. This study provides empirical evidence for understanding the differential impacts of built environments on OTC in coastal cities during summer, while offering actionable insights for urban planning strategies to enhance outdoor thermal environments and resident well-being. © 2025 Elsevier Ltd},
