	affiliations = {Faculty of Social and Economic Studies, University of J. E. Purkyně in Ústí nad Labem, Pasteurova 1, Ústí nad Labem, 400 96, Czech Republic},
	abstract = {Community gardens (CGs) provide a diverse array of ecosystem services (ES), contributing to the well-being of individuals and communities. However, their longevity depends not only on the active engagement of users but also on how CGs and their benefits are perceived by the surrounding community. Main aim of this study is to investigate how perceptions of the importance of ES and other benefits provided by CGs differ between CG users (those actively engaged in CG activities) and non-users (residents living near CGs but not participating). Data were collected through an on-site questionnaire survey among CG non-users in two Czech cities, Ústí nad Labem and Prague, and an online survey of CG users across Czechia. The findings reveal that while both groups valued multiple ES, non-users prioritised environmental benefits such as air quality improvement and crop production, alongside recreational functions. In contrast, CG users placed the highest importance on cultural and social benefits, particularly recreation, social interaction, and aesthetic enhancement, while giving lower priority to provisioning functions. These differences highlight the need to address diverse stakeholder perspectives when promoting CGs. A balanced approach that integrates environmental, social, and cultural considerations can enhance the appeal of CGs to both users and non-users, supporting their sustainable development and integration into urban environments. © 2026 The Authors.},
