	year = {2026},
	journal = {Transportation Research Part C: Emerging Technologies},
	volume = {190},
	pages = {},
	doi = {10.1016/j.trc.2026.105800},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105041664472?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {Key Laboratory of Road and Traffic Engineering of the Ministry of Education, Tongji University, Shanghai, 201804, China},
	abstract = {For autonomous vehicles (AVs) to operate reliably in real-world traffic, it is essential to evaluate the safety implications of their planned maneuvers by anticipating uncertainties in the future behaviors of other road users. To address this challenge, we introduce SURE-Plan, a Spatio-temporal Uncertainty-aware Reachable set Embedding framework for motion planning of AVs. First, we develop a deep evidential learning module integrated with a Normal-Inverse-Gamma prior, enabling simultaneous prediction of surrounding vehicles’ trajectories alongside quantification of both epistemic and aleatoric uncertainties through a single forward pass, thereby effectively capturing risks in long-tail scenarios. Subsequently, using these uncertainty-aware predictions, we exclude deterministically occupied regions from the reachable set of the ego vehicle. This process yields safe convex subsets at each timestep, which are concatenated into a spatio-temporal driving corridor to serve as hard constraints for subsequent quadratic programming. Finally, within the trajectory optimization module, we introduce an uncertainty-driven cost function that penalizes high-uncertainty regions while flexibly exploiting lower-uncertainty areas, thereby achieving a balance between robustness and efficiency. Evaluated on three public benchmarks-CommonRoad, SIND, and nuPlan-SURE-Plan maintains competitive performance over state-of-the-art baselines. Qualitative results further highlight its ability to produce adaptive and interaction-aware behaviors in highly interactive scenarios, such as unprotected left turns, making it a promising solution for safe and efficient motion planning in dense urban environments. © 2026 Published by Elsevier Ltd.},
