	abstract = {Urban parks are increasingly recognized as a critical component of sustainable urban development and human well-being. Understanding how greenspace landscape patterns influence users' perceptions of park attractiveness is essential for optimizing park design. In this study, we integrated large language models (LLMs) with geospatial analysis to investigate the spatial and temporal dynamics of perceived park attractiveness in Hong Kong. A fine-tuned GPT model was applied to over 68,000 user reviews from Google Maps to quantify attractiveness for 158 urban parks. High-resolution LiDAR-derived vegetation data was used to extract seven landscape metrics related to composition and configuration. We compared the patterns of perceived attractiveness between two time periods, 2017–2019 and 2021–2023, representing pre- and late-COVID-19 periods. The results showed a significant decline in perceived park attractiveness after the pandemic. Random forest modeling revealed that both the contribution and marginal effects of landscape metrics changed across periods. In particular, the proportion of greenspace, landscape connectivity, and diversity became more influential in the late-pandemic period, while landscape fragmentation exerted stronger negative effects. Partial dependence analysis further identified threshold effects and nonlinear responses in user perception. Our findings highlight the shifting public preference toward larger, more cohesive, and diverse greenspaces in the aftermath of the pandemic. This study proposes a novel framework for integrating LLM-based sentiment analysis with landscape metrics, offering new insights for data-driven and perception-centered urban park planning. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd},
