	abstract = {Urban jogging is widely recognized as a key component of healthy and active cities, yet its participation is often unequally distributed across different communities. Understanding the social inequities in jogging activity is critical for designing inclusive, health-promoting urban environments. This study investigates how neighborhood socioeconomic status (SES) and built environment jointly shape jogging activity, using a causal perspective. Proposing a novel big data causal approach that integrates trajectory clustering, causal machine learning, and econometric techniques, we explore 835,111 jogging trajectories from the Keep App alongside geospatial data from Hangzhou, China. Our results reveal that SES plays both direct and indirect roles in shaping jogging patterns, with residential self-selection amplifying disparities. For the built environment, design and diversity show threshold effects on both weekdays and weekends, while topology and subjective perception have a stronger causal influence on weekends. Additionally, our study suggests that the high-density intersections and overall density often promoted in walking studies may not support jogging behavior. These findings reveal the complex drivers of inequalities in urban jogging and offer actionable insights for urban planners seeking to promote equitable access to physical activity. By integrating Bayesian network-based causal inference with propensity score matching and endogeneity evaluations, this study delivers robust causal estimates and offers novel insights into the spatial mechanisms of human mobility beyond mere associations. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd},
