	abstract = {Urbanization represents a drastic alteration of natural environments and poses a major challenge to the fitness of native wildlife. Animals that establish in urban areas usually do so by modifying their behavior and/or life-history traits, with reproduction being a critical stage. The Southern Lapwing (Vanellus chilensis) is frequently found in urban environments of Argentina, yet key aspects of its breeding biology remain poorly understood. This study evaluated the effects of urban disturbance on breeding parameters, parental care and antipredator behavior in Southern Lapwings across urban areas that differed in human disturbance level in the southeastern Pampas. Breeding success was very low (12.5%), and our results indicate that breeding outcomes were more strongly associated with fine-scale nest-site characteristics, particularly the presence of fencing, than with urban disturbance level. Breeding behavior did not vary across disturbance levels, but were instead associated with specific local and temporal variables. Antipredator behavior, measured as flight initiation distance (FID), decreased as nests were closer to roads. Together, these findings suggest that fine-scale habitat structure is important to understand breeding outcomes in Southern Lapwings within urban landscapes, whereas broad, landscapescale disturbance categories have limited explanatory power for behavioral variation. Given the global expansion of urbanization, understanding its effects on native fauna is critical for effective urban planning and biodiversity management. © 2026 Elsevier B.V.},
