	abstract = {Climate change deteriorates the environmental vulnerability of urban neighborhoods, leading to more severe heat island effects, air pollution, and systemic environmental risk. The diversity of building forms, especially geometric shapes, is one of the important factors influencing the urban environment and its response to climate and, and this whose relationship warrants comprehensive understanding. However, a knowledge gap remains in the holistic consideration of multiple environmental variables and their interaction mechanisms under complex building shapes, which may constrain the effectiveness of building design and systemic environmental risk perception. This study investigated the synergistic effects of different building shapes (triangle, square, hexagon, octagon, and circle) and wind directions (0°-180°) on multiple environmental parameters of wind speed, air temperature and PM<sub>2.5</sub> concentration in a typical low-rise neighborhood by the simulation method. A comprehensive environmental risk assessment model for outdoor pedestrian activity areas was proposed to guide the building form optimization and environmental risk control. Results showed that an increased number of building shape edges and diagonal wind directions (45° and 135°) contributed to improving the overall environmental quality in the neighborhood. Under diagonal wind directions, circular buildings, as a favorable scenario, raised wind speed by 21 % and brought the average air temperature closer to that of the incoming airflow, and lowered the PM<sub>2.5</sub> concentration and overall environmental risk by approximately 70 % and 60 %, respectively, relative to the less favorable scenario of triangular buildings. Our findings can provide support for climate-adaptive urban design and the sustainable development of urban environment. © 2025 Elsevier Ltd},
