	abstract = {Home cooking is increasingly discussed as a potentially health-promoting practice, yet little qualitative evidence is available on how adults interpret cooking at home in relation to food choice, family care, and everyday prevention. This study explored lay people's perceptions, experiences, and motivations regarding cooking at home as an everyday practice to which they attributed preventive meaning in relation to diet-related disease risk within family life in Spain. A qualitative content analysis was conducted on open-ended responses collected through an online survey completed by 169 adults. The analysis followed a mixed inductive–deductive approach informed by qualitative content analysis procedures. Seven main categories were identified: Ingredient Sovereignty, Ethics of Care, Perceived Risk, Food Literacy, Health Investment and Practical Constraints, Perceived Safety and Kitchen Trust, and Transmission of Cooking Skills. Participants perceived cooking at home as a way to control ingredients, quantities, preparation methods, and adaptation to family needs. It was also interpreted as a relational practice of care, affection, and protection. Prepared, processed, and takeaway foods were often viewed as less transparent or less trustworthy, although their practical value in contexts of time pressure, fatigue, or limited skills was also recognised. Findings suggest that cooking at home was understood not only as an individual dietary behaviour, but as a situated family practice shaped by food literacy, trust, emotional meanings, structural constraints, and the transmission of cooking skills. Public health and food education strategies should therefore avoid idealising home cooking and instead support feasible, critical, and practically applicable culinary skills adapted to everyday living conditions. © 2026 The Authors.},
