	abstract = {The deployment of unmanned aerial vehicles (UAVs) in urban environments raises significant risk to the public, which is highly associated with weather conditions as well as time-variant population and vehicle densities. Since risk is inherently time-variant due to dynamic population and vehicle densities, risk evaluation becomes temporally coupled with uncertain waypoint arrival times (WATs), which are influenced by weather-induced uncertainties in UAV ground speed. Fortunately, integrating the path planning model with a high-resolution spatiotemporal risk map can select the risk-minimum path for UAVs and thereby reduce overall operational risk exposure. However, most existing path planning methods assume deterministic WATs and therefore cannot capture the coupled effects between time-variant risk and WAT uncertainty. To address this problem, a four-dimensional (4D) path planning framework is developed by extending conventional three-dimensional (3D) models to explicitly incorporate time, enabling the coupled analysis of time-variant risk and WAT uncertainty. A data-driven method is proposed to characterize time-variant risk uncertainty using electronic police surveillance data, and a time-layering four-dimensional space–time A* algorithm (TL-FDSTA*) is proposed to efficiently compute optimal paths. A case study in central Changzhou verifies the model’s capability to avoid high-risk regions while maintaining computational efficiency, achieving convergence within 116.7 s. The results demonstrate the potential of this research to support large-scale, safe, and efficient UAV operations in complex urban airspace. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.},
