	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105050400966?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {Universität Hohenheim, Institut für Sozialwissenschaften des Agrarbereichs, Fachgebiet Gesellschaftliche Transformation und Landwirtschaft (430b), Schloss, Museumsflügel, Stuttgart, 70599, Germany; Acker e.V., Büro Deutschland, Malzfabrik Bessemerstr. 2-14, Berlin, 12103, Germany; Integrative Research Institute Transformation of Human-Environment-Systems (IRITHESys), Research Group Multifunctional Landscapes, Humboldt-Universität zu Berlin, Unter den Linden 6, Berlin, 10099, Germany; Thaer-Institute of Agricultural and Horticultural Sciences, Humboldt-Universität zu Berlin, Unter den Linden 6, Berlin, 10099, Germany},
	abstract = {Semi-public residential green spaces are key components of urban everyday landscapes, shaping daily practices, social interaction, wellbeing, and access to nature, yet their social functions remain poorly understood compared to urban parks and community gardens. This study investigates how residents use and perceive residential green spaces in four neighbourhoods in Saarbrücken, Germany, comparing areas with and without community gardens. Using a mixed-methods approach combining structured surveys and 78 semi-structured interviews, we analyse patterns of use, perceived accessibility, social value, and interest in productive practices. Results show that residential green spaces are widely perceived as health-promoting and emotionally significant and are primarily used for walking, sitting, and socialising, while around one-third of respondents engage in vegetable cultivation or urban foraging. Although about half of respondents express an interest in gardening more, participation remains limited by structural, informational, and social barriers, including unclear access arrangements, limited knowledge, and communication challenges. Contrary to expectations, the presence of community gardens does not substantially increase overall use of surrounding green spaces but is associated with greater awareness of and interest in productive activities. Sociodemographic variables show limited influence, suggesting that spatial, institutional, and governance-related factors play a more decisive role. The findings reveal a persistent gap between residents’ interest in multifunctional green spaces and current design and management practices. We argue that residential green spaces should be rethought as inclusive, participatory everyday landscapes that support wellbeing through appropriate governance and long-term management. Future research should examine how different governance models and socio-spatial contexts influence long-term participation and collective stewardship in residential green spaces. © 2026 The Authors.},
