	affiliations = {Department of Landscape and Urban Design, Chaoyang University of Technology, Taichung City, Taiwan},
	abstract = {As key elements of green infrastructure, urban parks play a vital role in enhancing quality of life, supporting social cohesion, and promoting sustainable urban development. Research on these spaces has largely focused on the quality of their physical environments, however, with few systematic examinations of how civic engagement relates to a stable sense of place identity. Grounded in Service-Dominant Logic, this study examines how civic engagement, customer journey design (CJD), and collective psychological ownership (CPO) jointly predict place identity. Survey data from 485 Taiwanese urban-park users, recruited online, were analysed using three-step hierarchical regression. With demographic characteristics controlled, civic engagement significantly predicted place identity (ΔR² =.227, p < .001). Once CJD and CPO were entered, they explained a further 26.9% of the variance (ΔR² =.269, p < .001), the unique predictive contribution of civic engagement became non-significant, and CJD (β =.556) and CPO (β =.216) emerged as the dominant predictors; the final model explained 63.7% of the variance. CJD and place identity were closely associated (heterotrait–monotrait ratio =.894), so the relative magnitudes of these coefficients should be interpreted with caution. These findings suggest that civic engagement predicts place identity largely in conjunction with experiential design and collective ownership rather than on its own. Because the data are cross-sectional, they indicate where concurrent predictive strength lies rather than what interventions would produce change, and point to experiential coherence and formal participation as complementary rather than competing avenues for green-space governance. © 2026 Elsevier GmbH.},
	author_keywords = {Civic engagement; Collective psychological ownership; Customer journey design; Green infrastructure; Place identity; Urban parks},
