	abstract = {This study explores the relationship between responsive cohesion and aesthetic appreciation in the urban environment, using Istanbul's politically contested and symbolically charged Taksim Square as a case study. Drawing on responsive cohesion theory as an ethical framework, it focuses on two recent interventions, the Atatürk Cultural Center (AKM) and the Taksim Mosque, and examines how users evaluate these buildings and their wider urban context through a structured questionnaire. The findings reveal a significant association between perceived responsive cohesion and aesthetic appreciation, particularly in the more contested case of the Taksim Mosque, where lower aesthetic scores correspond to stronger correlations with responsive cohesion parameters. Additionally, the study reveals that aesthetic evaluations of individual buildings correlate meaningfully with perceptions of the surrounding streetscape, although architectural-scale appreciation does not necessarily translate into aesthetic appreciation at the urban scale. These results suggest that contextual responsive cohesion plays a central role in how users perceive architectural interventions in contested public spaces. By demonstrating how researchers can operationalise internal and contextual responsive cohesion across architectural and urban scales, the study contributes to responsive cohesion theory and broader debates on urban aesthetics, ethics, and the symbolic dimensions of urban design. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd},
