	abstract = {Street greening plays an important role in shaping human perceptions of urban environments. However, previous studies have mainly focused on overall greenness or static vegetation indicators, while seasonal effects of street greening remain insufficiently explored. This study develops a quantitative framework to evaluate the influence of fallen-leaf season street greening on ecological perception in central Shanghai by integrating Baidu Street View, semantic segmentation, questionnaire surveys, and regression modeling. A questionnaire survey involving 200 sampling locations was conducted to obtain ecological perception scores, and a mixed-variable regression model was constructed and validated using five-fold cross-validation (R<sup>2</sup> = 0.805; adjusted R<sup>2</sup> = 0.793). Results revealed that: (1) Several seasonal vegetation indicators exhibited interval-dependent effects; (2) Compared with the low-level category, proportion of deciduous tree (P<sub>DT</sub>) showed positive effects at medium levels but negative effects at high levels; proportion of yellow-leaved tree (P<sub>YT</sub>) showed negative effects at both medium and high levels, whereas proportion of colored shrubs and grasses (P<sub>CSG</sub>) showed positive effects only at medium levels. (3) Ecological perception showed a spatial gradient, with lower values in the urban core and higher values in peripheral areas, and was lower in Puxi than in Pudong. These findings highlight the importance of incorporating seasonal vegetation characteristics into street greening assessment. © 2026 by the authors.},
