	affiliations = {School of Civil Engineering and Transportation, Guangzhou University, Guangzhou, China; College of Landscape Architecture & Arts, Northwest A&F University, Xianyan, China; Key Laboratory of Coal Resources Exploration and Comprehensive Utilization, Ministry of Natural Resources, Shaanxi Coal Geology Group Company Limited, Xi'an, China; School of Civil Engineering, Chongqing University, Chongqing, China; State Key Laboratory of Safety and Resilience of Civil Engineering in Mountain Area, Chongqing, China},
	abstract = {Rapid urbanization has intensified urban heat exposure, reducing the usability and health safety of outdoor public spaces, particularly in hot-humid environments. Although misting systems are increasingly used as an active cooling strategy, field evidence remains limited on how operating parameters influence cooling performance and human thermal and physiological responses under real outdoor conditions. Field measurements were conducted in an outdoor open space in Guangzhou, China, from 1 to 11 July 2023 (daily maximum air temperatures: 30.8–36.6°C). A six-nozzle misting system was used to evaluate the effects of nozzle height (2.3, 2.7 and 3.1 m) and pressure (0.2, 0.3 and 0.4 MPa). The results showed that: (1) Misting expanded the range of perceived thermal neutrality, increasing the neutral UTCI range from 27.3–31.6°C under dry conditions to 28.6–34.2°C under misted conditions. (2) Misting activation increased the UTCI threshold for thermal unacceptability from 33.6°C before misting to 37.5–38.7°C across spray pressures and 37.3–38.6°C across nozzle heights. (3) Permutation importance revealed condition-dependent shifts in the contributors to overall thermal sensation: the forehead dominated under most spray configurations (47.17–66.24%), whereas chest became dominant under 0.4 MPa (44.15%) and 3.1 m (43.12%) conditions. (4) MST remained dominant before and after misting activation, increasing from 43.52% before misting to 46.62–77.47% after misting, followed by HR (18.51–42.54%), while ACT showed lower post-misting contributions (2.23–10.84%). These findings provide field evidence for optimizing misting parameters to improve outdoor thermal resilience in hot-humid urban spaces. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd},
