	affiliations = {Guangzhou Institute of Geography, Guangdong Academy of Sciences, Guangzhou, 510070, China; Department of Land Surveying and Geo-Informatics, The Hong Kong Polytechnic University, 999077, Hong Kong},
	abstract = {As the importance of child-friendliness gains global recognition, addressing how to enhance urban environments for children emerges as both a critical and complex challenge. Although previous research has explored the relationship between urban settings and human perception, these studies have not considered children's unique perspectives. This study pioneers an analysis of children's perceptions of urban spaces in comparison with those of adults. Utilizing image semantic segmentation on large-scale street view images (SVIs), we quantify the distribution of various urban elements, assessing aspects such as enclosure, openness, and greenness at the community level. Further, our study delves into the interplay among population, facilities, and perceptual systems in central and suburban areas. Our findings indicate that children are more attuned to lower-level urban elements than adults, resulting in a significantly diminished perception of openness among children. Additionally, children's perceptions do not always align with demographic trends or facility availability, suggesting that merely increasing the ratio of facilities per capita does not guarantee enhanced child-friendliness. These insights underscore the necessity of adopting a more human-centered approach to urban planning to foster environments that are truly conducive to children's well-being. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd.},
