	doi = {10.3389/frsc.2026.1840670},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105042355417?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {Engineering Systems Management, College of Engineering, American University of Sharjah, Sharjah, United Arab Emirates; Department of Industrial Engineering, College of Engineering, American University of Sharjah, Sharjah, United Arab Emirates; Department of Biology, Chemistry & Environmental Sciences, College of Arts and Sciences, American University of Sharjah, Sharjah, United Arab Emirates; Energy, Water and Sustainable Environment Research center (EWSERC), American University of Sharjah, Sharjah, United Arab Emirates},
	abstract = {Introduction – Urban livability has become a central component of sustainable development and quality-of-life (QoL) planning in rapidly growing cities. This study investigates the key urban livability dimensions influencing residents' perceived QoL in residential communities within the rapidly urbanizing United Arab Emirates (UAE). Methods – Employing a mixed-methods approach that integrates Fuzzy Delphi expert consensus and Structural Equation Modeling (SEM) of resident survey data, the research examines the influence of environmental quality, green infrastructure and ecosystems, transport and mobility, social and community services, living conditions of the built environment, and sustainability and resource management. Results – The findings reveal that green infrastructure and ecosystems, transport and mobility, and the living conditions of the built environment significantly enhance perceived QoL. Environmental quality also exerts a substantial effect, as poor air quality, excessive noise, and visual pollution negatively impact residents' wellbeing. In contrast, social and community services and sustainability and resource management do not emerge as significant direct predictors in this context. Discussion – These findings highlight the primacy of physical-environment and mobility factors in shaping livability perceptions within the UAE and underscore the need for resident-centered, evidence-based urban planning. Given that the analysis is grounded in a single-community sample, the findings are interpreted analytically and are intended to provide context-specific, exploratory insights for policymakers seeking to design sustainable and livable urban environments in the Gulf region and comparable rapidly urbanizing contexts, while signaling the need for larger, multi-community studies to confirm and extend these results. Copyright © 2026 Abdulmaksoud, Alherimi and Samara.},
	author_keywords = {fuzzy Delphi; green infrastructure; quality of life; residential communities; structural equation modeling; sustainable urban development; United Arab Emirates; urban livability},
