	affiliations = {Faculty of Engineering, Shahid Bahonar University, Kerman, Iran, Associate Professor, Pajoohesh Sq, Kerman, Iran; Centre for Accident Research and Road Safety - Queensland Queensland University of Technology (QUT), Kelvin Grove, 4059, Australia; Florida Polytechnic University, United States},
	abstract = {This study aims to identify the factors that threaten pedestrian safety in urban environments without just relying on traditional crash data. A multi-phase methodological approach is employed. In the first phase, two machine learning algorithms, Random Forest (RF) and Light Gradient Boosting Machine (LGBM), are utilized to determine the key variables influencing pedestrian safety, using crash frequency, near-crash frequency, and perceived safety as target variables. In the second phase, association rule mining is applied to identify patterns in self-reported pedestrian crash experiences. The third phase employs a text-mining technique, Term Frequency–Inverse Document Frequency (TF-IDF), to extract prominent keywords related to unsafe pedestrian behaviors from collected voice messages. Data for the first two phases were obtained through questionnaires, with responses from 371 and 189 participants, respectively. In the final phase, voice messages were collected from 328 participants. The findings emphasize the need to enhance pedestrian security to improve safety perceptions and reduce risky behaviors. Recommended interventions include improving infrastructure, increasing street lighting, and implementing traffic-calming measures. Additionally, education and the application of artificial intelligence technologies play a critical role in promoting pedestrian safety. Unsafe behaviors were identified as significant contributors to reduced walking safety, and the deployment of digital billboards is proposed as an effective strategy to mitigate such behaviors and improve pedestrian awareness and concentration. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd.},
