	abstract = {This study assesses the livability of peri-urban settlements in Akure, Nigeria, with an emphasis on their economic, social, and environmental dimensions. Data were mainly based on 245 structured questionnaires administered to residents, supplemented with direct observations. The mean index was used for the analysis, where the weighted values were ranked based on their importance. Responses were evaluated to determine the most important drivers of liveability, and were split into those that had a positive and negative impact on residents’ quality of life. Findings showed that economic activities comprising artisan, trade, and agriculture (farming), as well as civil servant services, are the main sources of income and drivers of the community’s resilience; however, low income earning limits access to basic services, thereby reducing the livelihoods of residents. Furthermore, social dimensions such as neighborhood amenities, kinship ties, hospitality, and cultural values contribute positively to cohesion and belonging, while inadequate utilities and services, poor public safety infrastructure, and marginalization undermine inclusivity and limit residents’ community participation. Environmentally, the physical state of the environment and plot sizes were satisfactory, while poor waste management, limited open spaces, and restricted access to electricity, healthcare, and education were identified as major contributors to the standard of living, exposing the residents to health hazards and limiting their opportunities for social interaction. The study recommends the implementation of targeted interventions to improve the livelihoods, infrastructure, social inclusion, and environmental sustainability in the peri-urban area. © The Author(s), under exclusive licence to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2026.},
