	affiliations = {School of Architecture, Southwest Minzu University, Chengdu, 610225, China; AI+Arch Lab, Southwest Minzu University, Chengdu, 610225, China},
	abstract = {Historic urban districts face substantial challenges in emergency evacuation due to constrained spatial layouts, strict heritage conservation requirements, and heterogeneous population compositions. To better characterize behavioral response mechanisms in such settings, this study proposes a cognition-driven evacuation modeling framework that simulates individual differences in spatial perception across varying cognitive levels, leading to differentiated behavioral responses in route choice strategy. AnyLogic model is developed associated with social force mechanism to simulate individual interactions. It integrates multiple variables including environmental factors, crowd congestion, and individual speed, to investigate behavioral heterogeneity during evacuation and its implications for system efficiency. The framework is empirically applied to the historic street network of Songpan Ancient Town, a representative case of a preserved cultural heritage environment in China. Results indicate that individuals with higher cognitive levels tend to select structurally clear and more efficient routes, exhibiting a reliance on route performance. In contrast, individuals with medium cognitive levels rely more heavily on salient visual cues and immediate perception, leading to more localized and passive route choice strategies that are prone to redundancy and efficiency loss; Individuals with low cognitive abilities rely on simple path choices and guidance from others. The three behavioral strategies exhibit significant differences in evacuation efficiency, sensitivity to congestion, and response variability, with performance disparities especially pronounced in long-distance segments and at bottleneck locations. The study contributes a scalable framework for modeling behavioral heterogeneity in constrained urban contexts and informs tiered evacuation guidance strategies that align with spatial structure and cognitive diversity, offering new insights for urban resilience planning in heritage zones. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd.},
	author_keywords = {Behavioral heterogeneity; Cognitive mechanism; Emergency evacuation; Historic urban environment; Route choice strategy; Social force model},
