	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105044801303?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {Universidad de Bío-Bío, Chile},
	abstract = {While the influence of neighbourhood on mental well-being no longer appears to be in dispute, more specific evidence reveals a relative consensus on the significance of factors such as the presence of green spaces, walkability, perceived urban integration, a sense of belonging to the neighbourhood, and its perceived social cohesion. However, most of these findings come from representative cases, which is why it seemed relevant to test these correlations in a unique case: a recognized heritage neighbourhood. Our hypothesis posits that, despite its unique nature, the aforementioned correlations would not only be confirmed but would even be strengthened by the awareness of living in a heritage neighbourhood, which is particularly evident in perceptions of belonging to the place and neighbourhood cohesion. To explore this, 110 surveys were conducted, assessing personal attributes, aspects of the built environment, urban habits, and self-reported perceptions of the neighbourhood and mental well-being. After statistical analysis —using a polychoric matrix and a multiple correspondence matrix— our results partially confirm the hypothesis: while the correlation and correspondence between mental well-being and perceptions of neighbourhood attachment, community cohesion, safety, urban integration, and the practice of walking area evident, the reported awareness of living in a heritage neighbourhood was only correlated with feeling integrated into the city. © 2026 Universidad de Chile. All rights reserved.},
