	abstract = {Street art has emerged as a vibrant form of expression that transforms urban landscapes, engaging pedestrians in ways that extend beyond mere visual appreciation. Although the cultural, artistic, and sociological significance of street art in urban settings is increasingly recognized, there is a lack of scientific studies examining its impact on pedestrian behavior. This study aimed to investigate the impact of street art on pedestrian behavior in a developing country. This study employed a qualitative approach, and semi-structured interviews were conducted with 46 participants who utilized footpaths with street art present. Using thematic analysis, the study found that street art significantly enhances the aesthetic appeal of these spaces, transforming footpaths into visually engaging environments that add vibrancy and serve as landmarks, fostering exploration and improving navigation. Street art fosters a sense of safety by promoting cleanliness and discouraging anti-social behavior. Well-maintained street art also creates a more secure, welcoming environment and evokes strong positive emotions, inspiring reflections on history, cultural identity, and a sense of connection to space. Future research could be advanced by conducting comparative analyses across cities to understand better how street art affects pedestrian behavior in diverse urban environments with unique cultural, social, and economic contexts. © The Author(s), under exclusive licence to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2026.},
	author_keywords = {Pedestrian behavior; Pedestrian perceptions; Physical space; Social space; Street art},
