	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105037033430?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {Department of Geography, Ghent University, Krijgslaan 281, Ghent, 9000, Belgium; Department of the Built Environment, Aalto University, Tietotie 1, Espoo, 02150, Finland; Department of Movement and Sports Sciences, Ghent University, Watersportlaan 3, Ghent, 9000, Belgium; School of Transportation, Southeast University, Jinxianghe Road 35, Nanjing, 210018, China; Department of Geography, University of Tartu, Vanemuise tn 46, Tartu, 51014, Estonia},
	abstract = {Population ageing and increasing urbanisation challenge cities to develop age-friendly urban environments that support older adults’ mobility, participation, and ability to age in place. Age-friendly urban planning strategies are guided by established policy frameworks, yet empirical insight into how older adults themselves prioritise neighbourhood interventions across different urban contexts remains limited. This study investigates older adults’ perceptions of their living environment to inform more context-sensitive, age-friendly urban planning. Using survey data from older residents of Ghent, Belgium, this study analyses 1318 responses to the open-ended question: ‘What would you change in your living environment to better suit your needs?’. A mixed-methods approach was employed, combining Biterm Topic Modelling (BTM) and inductive thematic analysis. BTM was used to systematically identify overarching patterns in short textual responses, while thematic analysis enabled a deeper interpretation of meanings and contextual nuances. The topic model identified four interrelated domains: urban traffic dynamics (33.44 %), public transport accessibility (27.41 %), public space and maintenance (20.22 %), and neighbourhood and social dynamics (18.92 %). Thematic analysis further revealed 44 themes within these areas, with public space and maintenance, especially sidewalk conditions, emerging as a major concern. The findings highlight the importance of accessible transport, high-quality infrastructure, and active participation of older adults in urban planning. The study demonstrates how mobility operates across policy domains within age-friendly urban environments. Methodologically, it demonstrates the value of integrating computational and interpretive approaches to capture both scale and lived experience in research on ageing and urban mobility. © 2026 Published by Elsevier Ltd. This is an open access article under the CC BY-NC-ND license. http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/},
