	abstract = {While sustainable transport is increasingly called for in urban environments to address congestion and pollution, the uptake of environmentally friendly modes of transport is still low because of implementation challenges and low incentives. Gamified green travel applications address this gap by integrating points, badges, challenges, and rewards into travel planning. In this study, the authors integrate the Theory of Planned Behavior (TPB) and the Technology Acceptance Model (TAM) and examine the intention of the passengers to use such apps. The study uses a survey data collected from urban residents to examine relationships between perceived usefulness, perceived ease of use, attitude, subjective norms, perceived behavioral control, enjoyment, incentives, and adoption intention, using structural equation modelling. The results indicate that perceived usefulness and ease of use improve attitudes and enjoyment and incentives improve perceptions. Gamification indirectly benefits adoption through usefulness and enjoyment, and social norms and behavioral control play a pivotal role in explaining adoption, which is in line with the integrated TAM–TPB framework. Copyright © 2026. Published by Elsevier Ltd.},
