	year = {2026},
	journal = {International Journal of Urban Sciences},
	volume = {30},
	number = {3},
	pages = {644 - 684},
	doi = {10.1080/12265934.2025.2591334},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105023464008?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {School of Architecture and Planning, Anna University, Chennai, India; Department of Civil Engineering, Anna University, Chennai, India},
	abstract = {Rapid urbanization, industrialization, uncontrolled population growth, and urban sprawl have led to land scarcity, forcing the conversion of Public Open Spaces (POS) into other land uses. This has increased urban density and reduced green spaces, exacerbating environmental issues such as climate change, pollution, urban heat islands, loss of biodiversity and soil contamination. Despite their vital role in enhancing environmental quality and urban sustainability, POS in both developed and developing countries often fall short in availability, allocation, and per capita standards, such as 9 square metres recommended by the World Health Organization (WHO) and 10 square metres by the Urban and Regional Development Plan Formulation and Implementation (URDPFI), when compared to national and international benchmarks. The lack of a rational approach to standard adoption, combined with ineffective design guidelines, limited public participation, and a predominantly supply-driven planning model, further undermines their effectiveness. This research introduces a novel supply-and-demand-based model that integrates user perceptions, behaviours, and both quantitative and qualitative insights to foster inclusive urban planning. A structured survey of 404 respondents was conducted in Chennai, India, to assess POS demand and influencing factors. Data were analysed using SPSS 25 and AMOS 26, applying Structural Equation Modelling (SEM) to explore relationships among key variables. Findings indicate that the quality of POS is the strongest determinant of user demand, followed by accessibility and quantity, suggesting that physical standards alone cannot adequately explain POS use. Unlike previous studies, this research comprehensively incorporates quality, quantity, accessibility, locality, and preference, offering a more effective approach to identifying demand drivers. The results demonstrate the need to transition from a supply-oriented to a user-centric planning model. This approach offers valuable guidance for planners, designers, and policymakers in optimizing POS distribution and use, even in land-constrained, high-density urban areas, supporting more sustainable and inclusive cities. Highlights Public open spaces, the lungs of cities, enhance livability and urban sustainability. Urbanization makes cities fail to meet standards using a one-size-fits-all supply rule. Research adopts a demand-based approach integrating quantitative and qualitative data. Structural Equation Modelling examines how user demand interacts with key factors. Observed and predicted results show strong standardized regression coefficients. © 2025 The Institute of Urban Sciences.},
	author_keywords = {Public open space; structural equation modelling; sustainability; urban planning; user-centric approach and climate change},
