	affiliations = {College of Landscape Architecture & Arts, Northwest A&F University, Yangling, 712100, China; Key Laboratory of Eco-Planning and Green Building (Tsinghua University), Ministry of Education, Beijing, 100084, China; Key Laboratory of National Forestry and Grassland Administration for Forest Therapy & Wellness, Yangling, 712100, China},
	abstract = {Understanding differential thermal perception mechanisms in elderly individuals with hypertension versus diabetes, and elucidating the multifactorial determinants of outdoor thermal comfort, constitutes a critical foundation for mitigating heat-related risks in vulnerable populations. This study investigated 287 elderly hypertensive or diabetic participants across three activity modalities (sitting, walking, and dancing) in typical urban park settings within China's cold climate zones. Through synchronous monitoring of microclimatic parameters, physiological markers, and psychological assessments, we identified significant disease-specific thermoregulatory phenotypes. Hypertensive (HTN) individuals demonstrated greater thermal sensitivity than diabetic (DM) counterparts, manifesting neutral physiological equivalent temperatures (NPET) of 31.3 °C versus 30.5 °C during sedentary activity, 22.9 °C versus 23.6 °C during ambulation, and 19.2 °C versus 18.7 °C during aerobic exercise. HTN participants exhibited consistently elevated local skin temperatures, particularly during sedentary and ambulatory phases, whereas DM individuals maintained higher heart rates across all activities with significant blood pressure divergence during walking. Psychologically, HTN cohorts reported superior positive affect, with significant differences observed during sedentary (P < 0.05) and aerobic (P < 0.001) conditions. Of the eight input configurations encompassing physical, physiological, and psychological factors, six attained test accuracies exceeding 75%, with HTN Dancing and DM ALL surpassing 80%. The integrated model for the DM cohort achieved maximal accuracy (80.8%).These findings delineate distinct thermoregulatory signatures between chronic disease cohorts and provide empirically validated tools for age-responsive urban landscape design and precision heat-health early warning frameworks. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd},
