	doi = {10.15666/aeer/2403_54075425},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105041100061?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {College of Fine Arts, Huaiyin Normal University, Huaian, 223000, China},
	abstract = {Vegetation in urban parks provides key ecological functions, including improving air quality, regulating the microclimate, and mitigating the urban heat island effect. Through seasonal changes and community stratification, it also influences visitors’ landscape experience and their evaluations of park use. Using Beijing, China as a case study, we integrate 574,459 social media reviews and remote sensing data from 114 parks collected between 2014 and 2025. Using these data, we construct seven vegetation feature variables that describe quantity, proportion, and coverage. Ridge regression is employed to identify the importance of these vegetation features. The regression coefficients are then used to construct the Vegetation Ecological Structure Index (VESI), which quantifies the structural characteristics of vegetation in urban parks. The results indicate that trees and overall vegetation coverage are the primary contributors to ecological functions. In contrast, shrubs and groundcovers play a supportive role by enriching community structure and improving environmental quality at the neighborhood scale. Time series analysis indicates that the spring flowering season and the autumn foliage season are key periods when vegetation shapes public perceptions. These seasonal fluctuations not only structure the temporal rhythm of the landscape but also heighten the public’s sensitivity to natural changes. The results reveal that the multidimensional characteristics of urban vegetation not only determine its ecological service capacity but also profoundly shape public affective perceptions. © 2026, ALÖKI Kft., Budapest, Hungary.},
	author_keywords = {ecological structure index; ridge regression; social media data; vegetation ecological structure; vegetation features},
