	abstract = {Urban villages (UVs), widespread informal settlements across Chinese cities, play a vital role in providing affordable housing; however, their livability remains underexamined. This gap hinders progress toward sustainable and equitable urban development. To bridge the gap, we propose a dual-scale livability assessment framework that integrates macro-level geospatial indicators with micro-level human perceptions of streetscapes. At the macro scale, we evaluate UV livability from four key dimensions: safety, health, convenience, and comfort, with indicators derived from multi-source geospatial data. At the micro scale, we incorporate human-centered visual assessments through a vision-language-model-based multi-agent framework, in which multiple agents interpret street-view images, generate rubric-based scores, and arbitrate scoring discrepancies to quantify perceived environmental quality. We choose Shenzhen, a megacity with diverse UV typologies, as the empirical case. Results demonstrate the effectiveness of the proposed framework in capturing diverse aspects of livability. The findings reveal a pronounced center–periphery gradient and location-specific trade-offs: centrally located UVs generally have higher convenience but lower comfort, whereas peripheral and mountainous UVs show the opposite pattern. Within the sampled UVs, exploratory evidence further suggests broad perceptual deficiencies in alley-dominated interiors, modest improvements in daylighting and openness associated with greater open-space shares, and broader but moderate advantages associated with commercial streets. The proposed framework provides a cross-sectional diagnostic tool for identifying spatial inequalities in UV livability and offers baseline evidence for differentiated urban renewal. It also holds promise for broader urban applications and contributes to the advancement of United Nations Sustainable Development Goal 11. Copyright © 2026. Published by Elsevier Ltd.},
