	abstract = {This article investigates the relationship between implicit familiarity toward natural and urban environments, and their impact on perceived restoration after performing mental imagery tasks. The research builds on the current debate on the determinants of restorativeness, exploring the roles of perceived familiarity, safety, and aesthetic appeal in restorative experiences of natural elements compared to urban ones. Two studies were conducted: the first on a representative UK sample (N = 314) and the second on a younger demographic (18–35 years, N = 170). Implicit associations between natural/urban elements and judgments of “familiarity” were assessed via the IAT (Implicit Association Test). Overall, the findings from both studies suggest that implicit familiarity may indirectly influence perceived restoration, mediated by explicit perceptions of familiarity and safety. Moreover, explicit judgements of aesthetic appeal emerged as significant predictors of perceived restoration. Engagement in natural mental imagery consistently demonstrated greater perceived restoration than urban mental imagery. Exploratory analyses in Study 1 indicated that age moderated these relationships, suggesting generational influences on restorativeness of environments. These generational effects were confirmed by performing Study 2 on a relatively younger demographic. This research highlights the complex interplay between implicit attitudes, explicit perceptions, and restoration, with implications for environmental design and psychological interventions. The findings underscore the importance of designing imagery interventions that emphasize safety, familiarity, and aesthetic qualities, with additional considerations the age of the recipients. These insights contribute to a nuanced understanding of how implicit and explicit factors shape restorative experiences and well-being. © The Author(s) 2026.},
