	abstract = {Contemporary management of urban green areas requires a comprehensive approach in many aspects. One of the key challenges is to design an aesthetic and attractive urban space while preserving maximum biodiversity. Hence, the objectives of this study were to (1) examine the relationships between visual attractiveness and species diversity, as well as (2) determine how plants’ biodiversity can be improved to be more attractive to the public. A landscape and species inventory were conducted for an urban park in Poznań, Poland. Based on this, the landscape was valorized, and biodiversity indices were calculated. Statistical methods were also applied to analyze relations, including PERMANOVA, the Kruskal-Wallis test, and PCA. The study confirmed that vegetation composition and vertical structure significantly influence biodiversity. Various levels of structure such as trees, shrubs, and low vegetation were important for both aspects studied. Landscape transformation affected both biodiversity levels and visual appeal, with pronounced effects in areas of low and high transformation. However, the high degree of management intensity in some areas promoted biodiversity and visual attractiveness. In the most visually attractive areas, numerous species with distinctive flower colors and leaf shapes forms were identified, potentially enhancing the subjective perception of the landscape. These findings are especially valuable for guiding the planning and design of urban green spaces, as they demonstrate how social expectations − such as aesthetic quality and recreational use, can be effectively integrated with environmental goals, particularly the conservation and enhancement of biodiversity. © 2026 The Authors},
	keywords = {Poland [Central Europe]; Poznan [Poland]; biodiversity; conservation management; greenspace; park design; species inventory; urban area; urban planning},
