	abstract = {In the face of the climate crisis, it is necessary to design multifunctional urban spaces that align with residents’ needs. The key to improving and ensuring the quality of life is to provide space for water and greenery, aligning with the principles of ecohydrology. Despite the importance of aquatic ecosystems in cities, the number of studies of the socio-cultural valuation of blue ecosystem services remains limited. Our study addresses this gap by surveying residents of Łódź, Poland, to understand how they value ecosystem services provided by reservoirs and rivers. The findings revealed that reservoirs were most valued in terms of services provided. Gender and interest or engagement in the environment were the main factors that influenced valuation of blue ecosystem services (BES). Moreover, people with higher education, women and older people gave the highest ratings to BES. Based on these findings, it is crucial to involve older citizens more broadly in actions related to blue-green infrastructure. Education and information dissemination about the benefits of freshwater environments are crucial, as greater eco-awareness correlates with broader recognition of these services. Tailoring water-related initiatives to specific demographic characteristics can further strengthen public engagement and support for sustainable water management practices. Recognizing the socio-cultural value of BES is vital for promoting restoration efforts and informing policies that integrate ecohydrological principles. By understanding public perception, we can enhance spatial planning, education, and management practices to create urban environments that are resilient, sustainable, and aligned with both human and ecological needs © 2024 European Regional Centre for Ecohydrology of the Polish Academy of Sciences},
