	affiliations = {School of Architecture & Design, Manipal University Jaipur, Jaipur, India},
	abstract = {Public open spaces are essential to social inclusion, well-being, and urban sustainability. Understanding how demographic groupings interact with public open spaces is still lacking in medium-sized towns, which are frequently disregarded in discussions about spatial planning. This study seeks to quantify the frequency and patterns of public open space use across demographic segments; assess perceived accessibility and usability among diverse user groups; and examine the relationship between user satisfaction and inclusivity features in medium-sized towns of Rajasthan. A systematic questionnaire was used to administer a cross-sectional survey to 1,059 inhabitants of three towns namely: Khairthal, SriMadhopur, and Neem-Ka-Thana. To investigate variations and correlations across user groups and spatial perceptions, data were examined using ANOVA, t-tests, Pearson correlation, and regression analysis. Age, gender, income, and education were among the demographic variables that had the most effects on public open spaces utilization-trends. Physical-mobility, mode of transportation, and proximity all affected perceived usability. There was a negative correlation between inclusiveness characteristics and user-pleasure, suggesting that inclusion by itself does not ensure contentment. The results highlight the necessity of user-informed, context-sensitive, and globally inclusive spatial planning. Mandating inclusive-design audits, strengthening gender-sensitive layouts, and increasing micro-mobility infrastructure in medium-sized towns are some of the recommendations. © 2025 Informa UK Limited, trading as Taylor & Francis Group.},
