	abstract = {Urban parks are some of the most used public spaces in cities across the globe. In rapidly urbanising countries like India, the safety of women in public spaces is a growing concern in line with the United Nations 2030 Sustainable Development Goals (5 & 11) mandate, which are targeted at safe, equitable and inclusive access to public spaces for all. This study seeks to fill the gap in firsthand knowledge about parameters that impact women’s perception of safety (WPOS) in urban parks of Indian cities. The method of enquiry involved walk-along interviews and observations. Interviews were transcribed verbatim, pseudonymized, and administered using NVivo 14 software. Our findings indicate two factors that influence WPOS: Firstly, environmental design and secondly, sociocultural factors. Gendered space theory highlights how these norms and socio-economic disparities are further intertwined with other factors in cities globally and not just in the Global South. © 2025 Informa UK Limited, trading as Taylor & Francis Group.},
