	abstract = {This study examined the mediating role of partner affection and support and maternal engagement in the associations between maternal and paternal depressive symptoms and children’s behaviour problems. Participants were 195 Guyanese couples and their preschool-aged children. Mothers and fathers completed a sociodemographic questionnaire, the CES-D scale, and an affection and support scale, and mothers provided assessments of their levels of caring for, playing with, and engaging in literacy-based activities, and children’s internalizing and externalizing behaviour problems. Maternal depressive symptoms were positively associated with children’s internalizing and externalizing behaviour problems, and paternal depressive symptoms were positively associated with children’s internalizing behaviour problems. Association between maternal depressive symptoms and externalizing behaviour problems was partially mediated through the mother’s perception of partner affection and support and her engagement with children. Family prevention and intervention programmes should focus more on couple relationship and parenting. © 2025 The Author(s). Published by Informa UK Limited, trading as Taylor & Francis Group.},
