	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105020848483?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {University of Warmia and Mazury in Olsztyn, Faculty of Geoengineering, Institute of Spatial Management and Geography, Department of Land Management, Olsztyn, Poland},
	abstract = {The dynamic growth of smart technologies is reshaping urban spaces and increasingly intertwines with the concept of Green City, which aims to balance sustainability and livability. Understanding how Generation Z – digital generation born between 1997 and 2012 – perceives smart infrastructure is essential, as they represent both current and future stakeholders in urban development. The objective of this study is to identify, which smart elements in public open spaces are most valued by Generation Z and how these align with the principles of sustainable Green Cities. The research was conducted in Olsztyn, Poland, which is a city with exceptional natural assets. Study employed a geo-survey method of 115 respondents aged 18–28 alongside an inventory of selected smart infrastructure components within the urban space. Spatial analysis in QGIS was applied to compare the actual distribution of smart solutions with users’ perceptions. Results show that mobility-related smart elements, especially intelligent pedestrian crossings and real-time public transport systems are mostly appreciated, being highlighted by over 70 % of respondents. In contrast, ecological and inclusivity-oriented solutions (e.g. EV charging stations, profiled pavements) were rarely indicated. Nearly 70 % of participants declared a general preference for smart over analog infrastructure, regardless of place of residence. The study concludes, that transport-related smart elements should be prioritized in sustainable urban investments, Smart Infrastructure and Green City concepts are complementary when carefully integrated, and that smart technologies can enhance urban attractiveness and resilience in Green Cities. © 2025},
