	abstract = {Our study categorized three spatial types—open space (OS), partially shaded space (PS), and fully shaded space (SS)—to encompass a spectrum of shading intensities. We engaged 48 elderly participants in a randomized tactile interaction with six distinct landscape materials: Sedum lineare, Stachys byzantina, Heptapleurum heptaphyllum, dimensional stone, antiseptic wood, and stainless steel. Subsequently, physical parameters were measured, a subjective questionnaire survey was performed and physiological parameters were monitored to ascertain how tactile perceptions in these outdoor settings influence thermal sensation and physiological indices among the elderly. Key findings included: 1) Elderly participants experienced naturalness, joy, relaxation, and comfort upon contact with plant materials. 2) Material type exerted a significant influence on the Palmar Thermal Comfort Vote (PTCV). Specifically, for S. byzantina and stainless steel, the Palmar Thermal Sensation Vote (PTSV) diminished as the shading degree of the space intensifies. Overall Thermal Comfort Vote (OTCV) was notably enhanced when touching dimensional stone in PS and H. heptaphyllum and S. byzantina in SS. 3) Systolic pressure decreased significantly when touching S. byzantina in SS compared to PS. In PS, β relative power and θ relative power were elevated during material contact, suggesting increased alertness and happiness. 4) OTCV correlated positively with tactile pleasure, in contrast to the PTSV. PTCV increased with enhanced perceptions of smoothness, pleasure, comfort, and relaxation. Blood pressure was lower with heightened feelings of naturalness and relaxation. δ power increased with greater tactile pleasure and comfort, while α power exhibited an inverse relationship. © 2025 Elsevier Ltd},
