	affiliations = {Universidad Pablo de Olavide, Spain; University of the Republic, Uruguay},
	abstract = {Wild boars were introduced to Uruguay in 1929 for hunting purposes. They are currently classified as a ‘pest,’ an ‘Invasive Alien Specie’ (IAS), and a primary target of hunting. However, they are also, occasionally, bred as pets. This study aims to understand, within the Anthropocene framework, how empirical animalities emerge through the spaces, bonds, and affections between humans and non-humans. To this end, we propose going through three animalities of the wild boar in Uruguay (enemy, rival and pet) resulting from discourses, perceptions, practices and management policies for the species. Adopting a phenomenological approach and the concept of fluid, intersubjective animality, we propose a supplementary analytical lens. We argue that the emergence of animality is not only relational (encounter-based) but also politically, socially, and affectively contextualised–through large-scale production models, micro-social practices such as hunting, and emotional needs–where interactions between species take place. © 2025 Informa UK Limited, trading as Taylor & Francis Group.},
