	doi = {10.1016/j.buildenv.2025.113362},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105010229433?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {Department of Architecture and Urban Planning, Shahid Beheshti University (SBU), Tehran, Iran},
	abstract = {Urban lighting plays a crucial role in people's moods and behavior during nighttime. Lighting design aims to enhance human well-being based on people's response to light. Virtual reality (VR) is a valuable tool for studying human perception under real lighting conditions. Research on human-centric urban lighting examines how lighting parameters impact perceptions and improve urban space quality. This study investigated various correlated color temperatures (CCTs) (3000 K, 5000 K, 7000 K), lighting techniques, and uniformity in VR-based urban experiments. Participants evaluated visual comfort, direction, feelings of security, visual field, and floor-level lighting while also evaluating the influence of lighting on human interaction and the amount of lighting in the space in terms of light-dark and glare-shaded. Forty subjects evaluated seven immersive virtual environments (IVEs) in three urban spaces using a 6-point Likert scale survey via an interactive user interface (UI). Findings indicate that altering the color temperature significantly affects visual comfort, perception of light, and glare. The highest satisfaction (77.5 % for visual comfort, 80 % for direction) was reported at 5000 K, while 7000 K led to the strongest perception of brightness (88 %) and glare (77.5 %). Also, changes in lighting technique significantly impact visual comfort, sense of security, visual field, influence of lighting on human interaction, light, and glare. Based on the findings, participants preferred planar lighting with uniform brightness for these aspects, respectively, 34 %,36 %, 26 %, 37 %, 41 %, and 22 % more than Accent lighting with non-uniform brightness. Participant feedback highlighted the VR lighting design as both realistic and engaging, demonstrating its effectiveness for urban lighting assessment. © 2025},
