	abstract = {Individuals make distinct walking choices based on the surrounding urban environment perceptions. Although a lot of work has been done in quantifying human perception of urban environment, existing research has not explored how the choices driven by perception can support walkability. This research aims to address this gap by proposing a framework that leverages 360-degree Google Street View Images (GSVIs), which serve as inputs to Deep Learning (DL) models. These models then extract informative features that contribute to the prediction of various perceptual attributes viz. beautiful, boring, safe, and lively collected through a primary survey. The attributes were integrated with road network at 100-meter intervals for the city of Mumbai, India. A multi-objective function is proposed to minimize the distance and maximize the perception to provide good routes. The DL models OCRNet and pretrained PPYOLOE + x achieved a high mIoU score of 72.13 %, and 51.4 %, respectively. Additionally, the state-of-art transformer-based model DINOv3 utilized for comparitive analysis and generalizability. The perception models also achieved a high accuracy of over 90%. Significant variations in routes were observed in diverse urban built-ups. The outcomes can support appropriate planning of urban spaces which can encourage walking, leading to reduction of carbon emissions. © 2025 The Authors},
