	affiliations = {College of Architecture and Landscape Architecture, Peking University, Beijing, 100871, China; Department of Parks, Recreation and Tourism Management, Clemson University, Clemson, 29634, SC, United States; Virtual Reality and Nature Lab, Clemson University, Clemson, 29634, SC, United States; Key Laboratory of Ecology and Energy-Saving Study of Dense Habitat (Ministry of Education), College of Architecture and Urban Planning, Tongji University, Shanghai, 200092, China},
	abstract = {Understanding how different groups perceive and evaluate urban environments is crucial for creating an inclusive, human-centered lifestyle. Despite its importance, limited research has examined the perceptual differences between design professionals and non-professionals in environmental evaluation. This study investigated how these two groups differ in their physiological, psychological, and perceptual responses to urban public space scenes with varying urban design characteristics. While physiological responses showed limited variation between groups, significant differences emerged in psychological responses and perceptual evaluations. Design professionals exhibited higher levels of arousal, engagement, and positive emotion but demonstrated lower interest compared to non-professionals. They also tended to be more critical in their environmental evaluations. These findings suggest that specialized knowledge acquired through professional education training influences multiple aspects of environmental perception, potentially shaped by socialization during professional education. Further research could explore the underlying mechanism of those professional-lay differences. This study contributes to developing virtual reality-assisted and evidence-based urban planning and design frameworks. © 2024 Elsevier Ltd},
