	affiliations = {Division of Computational Social Science, School of Humanities and Social Science, The Chinese University of Hong Kong, Shenzhen (CUHK-Shenzhen), Longgang District, Guangdong Province, Shenzhen, China; Department of Geography and Resource Management, The Chinese University of Hong Kong, Sha Tin, New Territories, Hong Kong; Institute of Space and Earth Information Science, The Chinese University of Hong Kong, Shatin, New Territories, Hong Kong; Department of Geography and Environment, Western University, London, N6A 5C2, ON, Canada; School of Automotive and Transportation Engineering, Hefei University of Technology, Hefei, China},
	abstract = {Urban parks are vital for health and well-being, yet equitable access remains elusive in topographically complex cities. This study evaluates 15-minute accessibility to urban parks for Hong Kong’s public housing residents by developing a novel measure that integrates a fine-grained 3D pedestrian network and subjective park quality from Google Maps ratings. Our analysis reveals significant spatial inequalities, which are systematically misrepresented by conventional 2D modeling. Key findings show that the 3D model increases the measured intra-urban inequality (Gini coefficient) by 20.8 % (from 0.4028 to 0.4867) compared to the 2D approach. The 2D model significantly overestimates accessibility in hilly districts; for instance, the median accessibility score in Wan Chai is 0.6128 units lower in the 3D model. Conversely, it underestimated accessibility in areas with grade-separated infrastructure. Hotspot and bivariate spatial analyses identified pronounced disadvantages for estates in topographically challenging districts such as Southern and Kwun Tong, where over 77 % of estates fall below the citywide median accessibility. The results demonstrate that achieving spatial justice in chrono-urbanism frameworks requires explicitly confronting vertical segregation, particularly for vulnerable populations in high-density cities. © 2025 Elsevier GmbH.},
