	affiliations = {School of Sport, Recreation, and Tourism Management, George Mason University, United States; Department of Recreation, Sport, and Tourism, University of Illinois Urbana-Champaign, United States; Department of Recreation, Park, and Tourism Management, The Pennsylvania State University, United States},
	abstract = {While urban parks are often positioned as multicultural recreation spaces bringing together racially and ethnically diverse visitors, there is mixed evidence regarding such contributions. A variety of factors can constrain or facilitate park visitation among diverse audiences; however, less is known about the factors that impact the diversity of visitors (i.e., mix of visitors from different racial or ethnic backgrounds). With their systems-level knowledge, park agency directors may be especially well positioned to identify socio-spatial constraints and facilitators of diverse visitation. This qualitative study included semi-structured interviews (N = 16) with U.S. urban park agency directors to assess their perceptions of the socio-spatial factors impacting park visitor diversity. Thematic analysis suggests three primary factors working in tandem via constraining and/or facilitating effects and impacts on park welcomeness: (a) legacy of segregation, (b) park condition/safety, and (c) park type/design. Findings demonstrate urban park directors’ understanding of historic and contemporary influences of visitor diversity. © 2025},
