	affiliations = {Research Group “Happiness by Design”, Department of Architecture and Planning, Indian Institute of Technology, Uttarakhand, Roorkee, India},
	abstract = {Stakeholder participation has become a crucial tool in urban planning processes, such as City Development Plans and Master Plans, addressing regional challenges to promote sustainability. This paper explores stakeholder perceptions of the relationship between urban environments and mental well-being, with a specific focus on the Indian context. A questionnaire was designed based on a literature review and expert opinions, comprising 49 physical environment indicators and 12 social environment indicators. The survey, conducted with 201 respondents, including urban residents, planners, and local stakeholders, used a 5-point Likert scale to gather responses. Principal Component Analysis (PCA) was employed to examine the perceived association between urban environments and mental well-being. Non-parametric tests were conducted to analyze how perceptions varied across different demographic groups. The findings reveal that ‘Greenspaces’ and ‘Social Connectedness’ were identified as the most significant factors impacting mental well-being. This study offers valuable insights into urban indicators that affect mental health and contributes to the development of a methodology for evaluating these impacts in urban settings. The results have significant implications for policymaking and urban planning, particularly in the Indian context, and can inform global efforts to create mentally healthy cities. © 2025 Informa UK Limited, trading as Taylor & Francis Group.},
