	affiliations = {School of Architecture and Urban-Rural Planning, Fuzhou University, Fujian Province, Fuzhou, 350108, China; College of Architecture and Urban Planning, Tongji University, Shanghai, 200092, China; School of Architecture, Southeast University, Nanjing, 211102, China},
	abstract = {Noise pollution has become an escalating environmental concern in urban areas. As essential natural components that deliver multiple ecological benefits, green spaces have attracted increasing attention for their potential to mitigate noise and foster tranquil soundscapes. Focusing on the core built-up area of Fuzhou City, this study integrates vector data on green space morphology with citizen noise complaint records to construct a tranquility gradient (T<sub>10</sub>–T<sub>100</sub>) based on 500 m × 500 m grid units. Through landscape pattern analysis and spatial statistical methods, three morphological metrics (Shape Index, Splitting Index, and Patch Richness Density) were examined to assess their associations on tranquility services. Correlation results indicate a statistically significant but trivial positive association between SPLIT and tranquility service quality, suggesting that moderately fragmented green spaces are, in this context, slightly associated with higher tranquility ratings in dense urban environments. Conversely, PRD shows a negative correlation, implying that higher typological diversity of green spaces is associated with lower tranquility service quality, potentially reflecting stronger co-location with human activity. SHAPE displays a weak negative association, indicating that morphological complexity alone does not substantially enhance tranquility. These findings underscore the context-dependent relationship between green space morphology and tranquility services, providing theoretical and practical insights for shifting urban green space planning from quantitative expansion toward morphological optimization. © The Author(s), under exclusive licence to Springer Nature B.V. 2026.},
