	affiliations = {College of Landscape Architecture & Arts, Northwest A&F University, Yangling, 712100, China; Key Laboratory of Eco-Planning and Green Building (Tsinghua University), Ministry of Education, Beijing, 100084, China},
	abstract = {This study investigated the effects of auditory-olfactory interactions on the psycho-physiological responses of elderly adults with hypertension in urban open spaces. Three typical park spaces in Xi'an, China were selected to create distinct auditory and olfactory environments. Subjective perceptions, physiological parameters, and electroencephalogram (EEG) signals were measured to evaluate responses in elderly participants stratified into high pulse pressure (HPP) and normal pulse pressure (NPP) groups. Key findings included: 1) Auditory stimuli influenced sound sensation and comfort votes in both HPP and NPP groups, while the interaction between auditory and olfactory stimuli affected sound sensation votes (p < 0.05). Olfactory stimuli influenced fragrance sensation and comfort votes in both groups, with fragrance pleasantness votes additionally affected in the NPP group (p < 0.05). 2) In the HPP group, natural sound (NS) reduced diastolic blood pressure (-4.435 mmHg) and heart rate (-3.519 bpm), while P. hortorum significantly lowered systolic blood pressure (SBP; -6.403 mmHg). In the NPP group, social sound (SS) significantly reduced SBP by 6.350 mmHg. 3) EEG analysis revealed greater sensitivity to auditory-olfactory stimuli in the HPP group, particularly in frontal lobe θ-wave activity, compared to the NPP group. 4) The NS & M. Canadensis combination achieved the highest health benefit score (0.521) in the HPP group, versus 0.456 for SS & C. obtuse in the NPP group. This study reveals how multisensory environments regulate psychophysiological responses in elderly hypertensive patients, advancing age-friendly urban design from “functional optimization” to “active chronic disease management” and providing evidence for health-promoting urban spaces. © 2025 Elsevier Ltd},
