	title = {Effects of environmental factors on multisensory perceptions of comfort, safety, and pleasure in autumnal urban riverfront spaces},
	year = {2025},
	journal = {Sustainable Cities and Society},
	volume = {131},
	pages = {},
	doi = {10.1016/j.scs.2025.106710},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105013319631?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {College of Landscape Architecture & Arts, Northwest A&F University, Yangling, 712100, China; Key Laboratory of Eco-Planning and Green Building (Tsinghua University), Ministry of Education, Beijing, 100084, China},
	abstract = {High-quality waterfront environments are crucial for healthy cities and sustainability. However, the links between environmental factors and residents' perceptions in these areas are not yet fully understood. This study addressed this gap by selecting 18 typical urban waterfront spaces within a large wetland ecological park in Xi'an, China during autumn. Using a “stop-and-go” approach, we combined environmental measurements (real-time and morphological data) with questionnaires to investigate how environmental characteristics of waterfront spaces influence residents' comfort, safety, pleasure perceptions, and overall perceptions. Results showed that: 1) Illuminance (LUX) and green view index (GVI) were the most significant environmental factors impacting overall comfort vote (OCV) and overall pleasure vote (OPV) respectively (p < 0.01), while only spatial openness (SO) significantly affected overall safety vote (OSV) (p < 0.05). 2) When air temperature (T<sub>a</sub>) was between 21.8°C and 31.8°C, thermal comfort vote (TCV) had the greatest impact on OCV; privacy safety vote (PSV) was the most important for overall safety; and naturalness pleasure vote (NPV) contributed most to OPV. 3) TCV and visual comfort vote (VCV) tended to have a one-vote veto effect on OCV, with NPV having the strongest one-vote veto effect on OPV, though not absolute. 4) Increased waterfront natural openness (WO) positively influenced overall perception, while illuminance (LUX) and physiological equivalent temperature (PET) had the opposite effect. OCV and OPV were key subjective indicators affecting overall waterfront space perception and had a strong veto effect on it. Our results offer practical guidance for creating high-quality waterfront spaces to enhance residents' health and well-being in sustainable cities. © 2025 Elsevier Ltd},
	author_keywords = {One-vote veto effect; Pleasure perception; Safety perception; Thermal comfort; Urban riverfront space; Visual comfort},
	keywords = {China; Shaanxi; Xian; Ecology; Sustainable development; Environmental factors; High quality; Multisensory perceptions; One-vote veto effect; Pleasure perception; Residents' perceptions; Safety perception; Thermal; Urban riverfront space; Visual comfort; air temperature; naturalness; nature-society relations; open space; perception; residential satisfaction; river; safety; urban design; wetland; Thermal comfort},
