	abstract = {This study investigates placemaking as a “queering” practice in historically stigmatized urban areas, particularly former mental asylums, focusing on Pionta Park, now partly a university campus. This research employs the “urban bench” as a metaphor and vector for queering public space, investigating the interplay between subjective experiences and material urban landscapes, and emphasizing the dynamics of inclusion and exclusion. Placemaking is seen as a transformative act that embraces fluid identities, diverse urban experiences, and new community forms. Through university-led participatory action research, this study provides visual and narrative insights into urban spaces, positioning placemaking as a dynamic, ongoing “queering” process. It reimagines urban spaces to enhance pedestrian flow and proposes a critical pedagogical approach for universities to cultivate queer futures within their communities. This study emphasizes how “queering” challenges rigid institutional structures, reshapes place perceptions, and redefines public space use. © 2025 Informa UK Limited, trading as Taylor & Francis Group.},
