	abstract = {This study explores the potential for integrating historical scientific heritage into urban landscapes by analyzing online search behavior related to the Astronomical Institute of the Romanian Academy (AIRA) park in Bucharest and its association with the adjacent Carol I Park. Comparative literature has shown that public interest in such heritage sites enhances urban attractiveness and supports sustainable development strategies. Accordingly, this research adopts a cultural-ecosystem analysis approach and employs a digital methodology to assess the online visibility and user interest profile of the AIRA park. Boolean search strategies were used to construct a comprehensive keyword database – including terms such as “Vila Bosianu” (Bosianu House), “Institutul Astronomic” (Astronomical Institute), and “Observatorul Astronomic București” (Bucharest Astronomical Observatory) – which was then expanded with related semantic variants using the GetKeywords and Google Ads platforms. The resulting metrics, processed and visualized in Microsoft Excel, provide an in-depth “X-ray” of current online searches conducted in Romanian and from within Romania via the Google search engine, focusing on these cultural-scientific landmarks. The analysis reveals a semantic association between the scientific and cultural objectives of the two parks – Carol I and AIRA – as well as a complementarity in their historical dimensions. Despite its limited public access due to its role as a scientific research site, the AIRA park generates significant online interest, comparable to that of some established attractions within Carol I Park. These findings suggest that bridging the current administrative and functional discontinuities between the AIRA and Carol I parks could foster a unified cultural hub, contributing to better preservation and valorization of cultural heritage, enhanced public access, and the reinforcement of Bucharest’s urban identity. By providing empirical data on users’ online search behavior, this research contributes to the strategic discourse on urban modernization and cultural integration. Furthermore, its innovative methodology advances socio-cultural territorial analysis and offers a robust framework for assessing the interplay between digital public engagement and urban heritage. © 2025, Publishing House of the Romanian Academy. All rights reserved.},
	author_keywords = {Carol I Park; cultural heritage; online search metrics; place identity; public perception; scientific patrimony; search engine optimization; sustainable management; urban integration; Vila Bosianu},
