	abstract = {With the continuous proliferation of shared bicycles, the overall development of the bike-sharing industry is transitioning from extensive market deployment to refined scenario operation. Concurrently, the campus bike market is gradually emerging as a significant segment. Although previous studies have explored the usage characteristics of bike-sharing systems in urban environments, the understanding of bike-sharing systems within large-scale campuses, which do not permit riding outside the campus, remains incomplete. To explore the determinants of students’ satisfaction with campus dockless bike-sharing system, a survey of students’ satisfaction was conducted in a large-scale campus in Tianjin, China. Exploratory factor analysis is applied to extract five common satisfaction factors out of 20 questionnaire items. Then, structural equation model is estimated to reveal the factors affecting students’ satisfaction with campus dockless bike-sharing system. Results show that the variable of bicycle situation & safety of the riding environment dominates the positive effects on students’ satisfaction with the dockless bike-sharing system. The bicycle quality and bicycle design are the key aspects need to be further improved. The study findings also reveal that students’ perception of health positively influences their satisfaction with bike-sharing services. Delivery scheduling and functional requirements are surprisingly found to be non-significant factors. The findings can help bike-sharing operators enhance service quality and help university administrators issue practical interventions to improve the efficiency and safety of students using shared bicycles. © 2024 Fabrizio Serra Editore Srl. All rights reserved.},
