	affiliations = {University of Massachusetts Amherst, United States},
	abstract = {Gustav Lange (1937–2022) was one of the leading German landscape architects in the 1990s. In comparison with other creative contemporaries, his work is less discussed and documented. Mauerpark Berlin, located on the former border strip of the Berlin Wall, was designed from 1992 to 2020 and is his biggest and most significant work. This study proposes an analysis of the design composition of Mauerpark at multiple scales. On the larger scale, Lange employs an axially oriented organization around primary geometric shapes, resulting in a stabilizing design vocabulary. On the smaller scale of the site, geometries and lines are broken up and increase the perception of dynamism. This paper documents critical stages of the now more than thirty-year history of the park, including narratives, plans and publications. It is hoped that this will help catalyse more reflection and discourse on Gustav Lange and his oeuvre. © 2025 European Council of Landscape Architecture Schools (ECLAS).},
