	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105006982791?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {College of Engineering, University of Baghdad, Iraq},
	abstract = {Natural and urban environments can potentially support psychological restorative experiences, such as restoring health from stress, improving focus and concentration, and enhancing cognitive functions. These environments are more likely to support such experiences that contribute to a sense of coherence as a quality scale for restoration. University campuses are one of the most important environments for restoration experience. The vast urban spaces of green areas, designed with urban qualities, create stimulating environments for students and teachers that attain attention restoration and stress reduction. This paper investigates the restoration experience for students and teachers at the University of Baghdad campus. The paper extracts a theoretical framework for the sense of coherence and restoration qualities introduced by psychological and urban theorists. Then the framework is applied to the campus building design and structure articulation, in order to explore the coherence and the restoration qualities. The paper follows an analytical descriptive approach to analyse structure articulation enhanced by urban theorists, and Alexander’s model of wholeness with fifteen characteristics as structure coherence leads to restoration experience. A questionnaire-based survey has been conducted in this study. The questionnaire has aimed to test the occupants' ability on the campus of the University of Baghdad to feel restoration by Restorative Perception Factor Scale questions as a sense of coherence, which have been asked through a set of inquiries. The research has concluded that most of the geometrical characteristics of the concept of coherence are included in the campus space design especially gradient and hierarchy in order to organize the complex system. Additionally, the results of the occupants' restorative experience questionnaire regarding the sense of coherence indicate that the selected sample had strong psychological restoration that supports post-occupancy evaluation and reveals an evidence-based design for a new habitat or a building layout design. The restoration experience is wholeness, the continuous experience of overlapping centers nodes, and districts with linking paths gives a sense of place as a restoration phenomenon to promote health and wellbeing. For most urban design theorists, the sense of coherence and the structured mental image are similar. © 2025 Praise Worthy Prize S.r.l.-All rights reserved.},
