	abstract = {Perception is the process by which an individual interprets environmental stimuli into something meaningful to him/her (Lawless, 1990; Pickens, 2005). Perception has three distinct forms, which are sensory, cognition, and affection. In normal circumstances, an individual uses his/her five basic senses (sight, sound, smell, taste, and touch) to gather information about an object or a phenomenon. These initial sensory impressions are then aggregated to form certain knowledge and beliefs about the same object or phenomenon, or cognitive perception (Hultén et al., 2009; Krishna, 2009). Such knowledge and beliefs later help define the feelings that the individual has of the object or phenomenon, or the affective perception (Baloglu and McCleary, 1999; Echtner and Ritchie, 2003). © 2021. e-Review of Tourism Research. All Rights Reserved.},
