	affiliations = {Department of History of Art, University of the Witwatersrand, Johannesburg, South Africa},
	abstract = {It was once customary to begin an inaugural address by paying tribute to the previous incumbent of the chair. Perhaps this practice has fallen into disuse here because, at a growing university like this one, many professors are appointed to new chairs in new departments. This is certainly the case in the Department of Art History, but none the less I do have a predecessor. I refer to my teacher, mentor and friend, Professor Heather Martienssen, who first introduced me to the joys of scholarly discourse and debate, a privilege I have continued to enjoy with colleagues, past and present, to whom also I wish to express my gratitude. Professor Martienssen established the Department of Fine Arts at this university and was the first woman appointed to a chair here. Within the Fine Arts training, she developed the discipline of Art History, although it was inaugurated as a separate department only after her retirement. It is not possible to describe the extent of the debt owed her by South African art historians and it is thus a very small token of my esteem and affection to dedicate this lecture to her. I like to believe that she would have approved of the topic, for her own inaugural address, the first in the Department of Fine Arts, was also concerned with visual exploration, and her great gift to those who studied under her was an awareness of the possibilities of formal analysis in the interpretation of works of painting, sculpture and architecture. © 1986 Taylor and Francis Group, LLC.},
