	abstract = {The planning of parks and open space in Canadian cities has been a fragmented process lacking consistent goals and motivation. Although a few cities have developed excellent plans that reflect both their natural settings and the particular needs of their citizens, until recently most had either no plans or had based their plans on the adoption of simplistic standards for the ratio of park space to population size. The paper describes a sequence of research studies undertaken to provide guidelines that would assist municipalities in Canada to develop sound planning procedures for an open space system. Since it was recognized that planning must be based on a sound understanding of what is being planned, and to what purpose, an important part of the research was devoted to uncovering the perceptions people have about urban open space. Other parts of the research studied the planning process itself, including the importance of open space planning relative to other urban issues. Some thirty-one guidelines were developed, addressing both the process and the substance of open space planning. The paper attempts to show a synopsis of the link between the perception of open space in Canadian cities and the way in which an effective planning process may be set up to meet the needs of individual cities and towns. © 1979 Taylor & Francis Group, LLC. All rights reserved.},
