	abstract = {The paper focuses on the micro approach of a complex research by analysing some of the international and Hungarian models of residential choice and considering the place of the urban environment in these models. In addition, it describes the findings of a survey on the issue conducted in Budapest by the author. The main hypothesis is that a rise in the social position causes a shift in the perception of the quality of the urban environment, which means that it becomes the prime factor in the selection of the next target area. However, the number of those in Hungary who change residence mainly for the reason of a better environment is still less than in western societies and the age when it affects the choice appears later in the course of life. According to the life course theory at certain stages of one's life different sets of driving forces generate changes in residence (Mulder 1993). However, owing to the deficiencies of the institutional, banking and welfare systems the theory is applicable with a shift in time at this stage of economic and social transformation in Hungary. The issue of housing prices is still a restricting factor, narrowing the scope of choice. Nevertheless, there is a significant number of people rising in their social position and wealth who take part in the late-coming modern form of sub-urbanisation process. To answer the questions arising in the study we need to look at the characteristics of the search areas (urban environments) as such, the circumstances that make a residential neighbourhood more attractive for certain households with respect to their needs and social background. We also have to consider the residential relocation process - starting from the decision to move, followed by the decision to view alternatives and ending in the actual relocation behaviour - focusing mainly on the place of the perception of urban environment in the studied models.},
