	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/0026303548?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Examines the project of sanitising the colonial city of Singapore during the late 19th and early 20th centuries as a dialectical encounter between the municipal authorities of the city and the Asian plebeian classes. By the late 19th century, the overwhelming concern for a sanitised environment in Singapore provided much of the ideological impetus which led to the passing of a ramifying web of municipal by-laws to regulate the daily routines and habits of the Asian plebeian classes. The success of the sanitary project, however, did not depend solely on the capacity of colonial institutions to control and regulate the urban environment, but also on the response and "strategic conduct' of the colonised to the new regime of disciplinary techniques. This paper first examines colonial perceptions of death and disease in Singapore and the emergence of a campaign to sanitise the environment as a panacea to alleviate the dismal conditions of health prevalent in the city. Contemporary perceptions of the aetiologies of disease were crucial in shaping the essentially environmentalist thrust of municipal schemes to improve public health. The rest of the paper examines the strategies of control devised by the municipal authorities to secure the sanitation of the city and considers Asian attempts at imposing their own counter-view with regard to health, disease and the management of the urban environment. -from Author},
