	affiliations = {United States},
	abstract = {This thesis derives a methodology for studying sense of place that combines ethnographic techniques borrowed from anthropology, sociology, folklore studies, humanistic psychology, and cultural geography. This methodology, applied on the Kansas High Plains, proved effective in extracting and describing sense of place. Results show the human experience of the High Plains to have a fullness belied by the landscape's austerity. Residents express great love for their way of life and profound attachment to the landscape, which often inspires them with feelings of freedom and peacefulness. The majority would not choose to live elsewhere. They are fond of open space, emptiness, and quiet, and averse to any sort of clutter, whether natural or cultural. Aspects of the High Plains sense of place include a lingering frontier mentality, fierce regional loyalty, friendliness, self-reliance, humility, insularity, slow pace of life, contempt for the eastern/urban establishment, greater geographical and spatial awareness, and a sense of isolation. -from Author},
