	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/0026380943?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {At one extreme we have "the city', with powerful, antagonistic combinations of filth, pandemonium, corruption and gluttony, whilst also being the only true place of civilisation. At the other extreme, "the countryside', an arcadian landscape of fields, flowers and sunshine. The countryside is seen as a place inhabited by simple, needy but nonetheless cheery folk, leading a genuine life of tranquillity, purity and worth. Many influential and enduring images and perceptions of urban and more particularly rural environments abound in popular culture. Most of us have definite notions about what constitutes a typical urban or typical rural environment. The question is how far do these ideas reflect a reality, and where do these ideas come from? This paper illustrates quite definitely the profusion of messages about urban and rural environments and the underlying ideology embodied in popular culture. There is no doubt that such images exist. However, what now warrants further attention and which as yet I can only infer, is the influence such messages have over our understanding, attitudes towards and experience of such environments. -from Author},
