	abstract = {This article argues that besides the analytical cognitive processing of the leftbrain, and the intuitive cognitive processing of the right-brain, there are other psychological aspects of problem solving that must be taken into consideration and planned for in mathematics classrooms if learners are to become competent problem solvers. These psychological factors, in addition to cognition, are: Volition, perception, affection, and psychomotor potentialities. To release these potentialities during problem solving, the author argues that the whole brain must be brought into balance (synchronization). This balancing act will necessitate that: (1) the left and right hemispheres of the brain be brought into synergistic union to aid in cognitive processing; (2) the back and front regions be brought into balance to aid perception (back half of the brain), psychomotor, cognition and volition (front half of the brain); and (3) the top and bottom regions (MacLean's Triune Brain) be brought into synch to insure that affection (a subcortical potentiality) positively impacts on the four aforementioned cortical potentialities. The author concludes the article by suggesting to teachers how they can arrange environments and guide interaction to produce more competent problem solvers. Throughout the article, the author uses characters from St Exupery's book The Little Prince to help her make the point that mathematics matters do matter.. not as 'matters of fact' or bottom-line 'matters of consequence', but rather as 'matters of so much importance' to children coming to know themselves and their worlds by 'doing things they feel are consequential' with personally generated mathematical understandings. © 1987 Taylor and Francis Group, LLC.},
