	abstract = {A collection of papers based on recent empirical research into aspects of recreational land use in Ontario. After the editor's introductory paper - and overview of the demand, supply and impact characteristics of outdoor recreation - the readings are arranged into six sections. The first concerns aspects of tourist and recreational supply: the Ontario Recreation Supply Inventory and the Ontario Provincial Park Classification are described; while other papers examine the recreational implications of climate, resource typologies, and accessibility to urban open space. The next section on recreation and tourist demand comprises an overview of the demand for outdoor recreation in Ontario and an examination of clusters of outdoor recreational activities. This is followed by four chapters linked by a common concern with the perception and attitudes of recreationalists. Subjects covered include children's use of urban play space, the preferences of visitors to Point Pelee National Park, and the influence of weather on park visitation. Patterns of recreational land use is the concern of section four. One contribution traces the historical development of Muskoka as a tourist area in the late 19th century, while others form case studies of Algonquin Park, downhill skiing, leisure plots, the Ontario Place entertainment complex, and the Oktoberfest beer festival. The two remaining chapters in this section analyse recreational trip generator and the phenomena of the recreational business district. Section five consists of four chapters on the impact of outdoor recreation: the first focuses on the ecological effects of snowmobiles, the second on the income and employment effects of tourism in Muskoka, the third on recreational crime in Ontario's Provincial Parks, and the fourth on social antagonisms created by tourism in Old Niagara. The final section is devoted to various planning and management issues ranging from waterside second homes to the limits of cost benefi